window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert11-chem-bonding","kind":"practice","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"title":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure · Practice","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","subject":"Chemistry","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-001","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":1,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Taking the z-axis as the internuclear axis, the $2p_z$ orbital of one atom can combine to form molecular orbitals with which orbital of the other identical atom?","image":"","options":["Only $2p_x$","Only $2p_y$","Only $2p_z$","Any of $2p_x$, $2p_y$ or $2p_z$, since they have equal energy"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Combining AOs must have the same symmetry about the molecular axis.\nThe $2p_z$ orbitals of both atoms point along the axis and share the same symmetry, so they combine to give $\\sigma 2p_z$ and $\\sigma^* 2p_z$.\nThe $2p_x$ and $2p_y$ orbitals have the same energy as $2p_z$ but a different symmetry, so they cannot combine with it.\nThis is why option 4 fails: equal energy alone is not enough.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-002","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":2,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"The dipole moment of water is 1.85 D. Taking 1 D = $3.33564 \\times 10^{-30}$ C m, what is its dipole moment in units of $10^{-30}$ C m?","image":"","options":["6.17","5.55","1.80","0.555"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\mu$ = 1.85 D and 1 D = $3.33564 \\times 10^{-30}$ C m.\nFormula: $\\mu$ (C m) = $\\mu$ (D) $\\times 3.33564 \\times 10^{-30}$.\nSubstituting: $1.85 \\times 3.33564 = 6.17$.\nSo $\\mu = 6.17 \\times 10^{-30}$ C m.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-003","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":3,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The carbon–carbon bond length decreases in the order ethane > ethene > ethyne.\n(ii) In ethene, the H–C–H bond angle is exactly 120° because each carbon is sp$^2$ hybridised.\n(iii) The π bond in ethene lies in the plane containing the carbon and hydrogen atoms.\n(iv) In ethyne, each carbon has two unhybridised 2p orbitals, which form two π bonds by sidewise overlap.\n(v) Each C–H bond in ethane is an sp$^3$–s sigma bond.\n(vi) According to MO theory, the double bond in the $C_2$ molecule consists of one σ bond and one π bond.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the C–C distances are 154 pm (ethane), 134 pm (ethene) and 120 pm (ethyne).\n(ii) Incorrect: the actual H–C–H angle in ethene is 117.6°, not exactly 120°.\n(iii) Incorrect: the π electron clouds lie above and below the molecular plane, not in it.\n(iv) Correct: sp-hybridised carbon keeps 2p$_y$ and 2p$_z$ unhybridised, and these form two π bonds.\n(v) Correct: each C–H bond in ethane is formed by sp$^3$–s overlap.\n(vi) Incorrect: in $C_2$, the four bonding electrons occupy two π MOs, so both bonds are π bonds.\nCorrect statements: 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-004","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":4,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (molecule) with List-II (shape):\nList-I\n(A) $SF_4$\n(B) $ClF_3$\n(C) $BrF_5$\n(D) $XeF_4$\nList-II\n(i) Square planar\n(ii) T-shape\n(iii) See-saw\n(iv) Square pyramidal","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) $SF_4$ has 4 bond pairs and 1 lone pair (AB4E), giving a see-saw shape: (iii).\n(B) $ClF_3$ has 3 bond pairs and 2 lone pairs (AB3E2), giving a T-shape: (ii).\n(C) $BrF_5$ has 5 bond pairs and 1 lone pair (AB5E), giving a square pyramidal shape: (iv).\n(D) $XeF_4$ has 4 bond pairs and 2 lone pairs (AB4E2), giving a square planar shape: (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-005","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":5,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Vinylacetylene has the structure $HC\\equiv C-CH=CH_2$ (carbons numbered 1 to 4 from left to right). Which statement about it is correct?","image":"","options":["It has 2 sp and 2 $sp^2$ carbons, and a total of 7 $\\sigma$ and 3 $\\pi$ bonds.","It has 2 sp and 2 $sp^2$ carbons, and a total of 6 $\\sigma$ and 4 $\\pi$ bonds.","C2 and C3 are both $sp^2$, and there are 7 $\\sigma$ and 3 $\\pi$ bonds.","The C2–C3 sigma bond is formed by $sp^2$–$sp^3$ overlap, and there are 8 $\\sigma$ and 2 $\\pi$ bonds."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1, hybridisation: C1 and C2 are joined by a triple bond, so both are sp. C3 and C4 are joined by a double bond, so both are $sp^2$.\nStep 2, sigma bonds: there are 4 C–H bonds (one on C1, one on C3, two on C4) and 3 C–C bonds, giving 7 $\\sigma$.\nStep 3, pi bonds: the triple bond has 2 $\\pi$ and the double bond has 1 $\\pi$, giving 3 $\\pi$.\nStep 4: the C2–C3 bond forms from sp–$sp^2$ overlap, which rules out option (4).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-006","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":6,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (molecule) with List-II (shape):\nList-I: (A) $SF_4$ (B) $ClF_3$ (C) $BrF_5$ (D) $PCl_5$\nList-II: (i) T-shaped (ii) trigonal bipyramidal (iii) see-saw (iv) square pyramidal","image":"","options":["A-iii, B-i, C-iv, D-ii","A-i, B-iii, C-iv, D-ii","A-iii, B-i, C-ii, D-iv","A-iv, B-i, C-iii, D-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) $SF_4$ is AB4E. The lone pair sits in an equatorial position, giving a see-saw shape, so A-iii.\n(B) $ClF_3$ is AB3E2. Both lone pairs are equatorial, giving a T-shape, so B-i.\n(C) $BrF_5$ is AB5E. The sp³d² arrangement with one lone pair gives a square pyramid, so C-iv.\n(D) $PCl_5$ is AB5 with no lone pair, giving a trigonal bipyramid, so D-ii.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-007","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":7,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Among the species $O_2$, NO, $NO^+$ and $O_2^{2-}$, which one has the shortest bond and the highest bond enthalpy?","image":"","options":["$O_2$","NO","$NO^+$","$O_2^{2-}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Count the electrons in each species to find its bond order.\n$NO^+$: 7 + 8 − 1 = 14 electrons. It is isoelectronic with $N_2$ and CO, so its bond order is 3.\nNO: 15 electrons, so its bond order is 2.5 (one electron in an antibonding π* orbital).\n$O_2$: 16 electrons, bond order 2.\n$O_2^{2-}$: 18 electrons, isoelectronic with $F_2$, bond order 1.\nHigher bond order means a shorter and stronger bond, so $NO^+$ fits.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-008","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":8,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Isoelectronic molecules and ions have identical bond orders. Which of the following species has the same bond order as $F_2$?","image":"","options":["$O_2$","$O_2^{-}$","$O_2^{2-}$","$N_2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"$F_2$ has 18 electrons and a single bond, so its bond order is 1.\n$O_2^{2-}$ has $8 + 8 + 2 = 18$ electrons. It is isoelectronic with $F_2$, so its bond order is also 1.\n$O_2$ has bond order 2. $O_2^{-}$ has bond order 1.5. $N_2$ has bond order 3.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-009","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":9,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"How does the magnitude of hydrogen bonding in a compound depend on its physical state?","image":"","options":["Gas > liquid > solid","Liquid > solid > gas","Solid > liquid > gas","It is the same in all three states"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the magnitude of hydrogen bonding is maximum in the solid state and minimum in the gaseous state.\nIn the solid state the molecules are held closest and in a fixed arrangement, so the most hydrogen bonds are formed.\nIn the gaseous state the molecules are far apart, so hydrogen bonding is least.\nThis gives the order solid > liquid > gas, which rules out the other options.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-010","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":10,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In the $N_2$ molecule, the $\\sigma 2p_z$ molecular orbital is higher in energy than the $\\pi 2p_x$ and $\\pi 2p_y$ molecular orbitals.\nStatement II: In the $F_2$ molecule, the $\\sigma 2p_z$ molecular orbital is lower in energy than the $\\pi 2p_x$ and $\\pi 2p_y$ molecular orbitals.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. For $B_2$, $C_2$ and $N_2$ the order is $(\\pi 2p_x = \\pi 2p_y) < \\sigma 2p_z$, so $\\sigma 2p_z$ lies above the $\\pi$ MOs.\nStatement II is correct. For $O_2$ and $F_2$ the order is $\\sigma 2p_z < (\\pi 2p_x = \\pi 2p_y)$.\nBoth energy orders were established experimentally from spectroscopic data.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-011","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":11,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Simple overlap of pure atomic orbitals predicts H–N–H and H–O–H angles of 90° in $NH_3$ and $H_2O$, but the observed angles are 107° and 104.5° respectively.\nStatement II: Simple overlap of the four half-filled orbitals of excited carbon ($2s^1 2p_x^1 2p_y^1 2p_z^1$) with H 1s orbitals predicts four C–H bonds directed at 109.5° to one another.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Bonds formed by the mutually perpendicular p orbitals of N or O should be at 90°. This disagrees with the actual angles of 107° and 104.5°.\nStatement II is incorrect. Overlap with the three 2p orbitals gives three C–H bonds at 90°. The fourth bond uses the spherical 2s orbital, so its direction cannot be fixed. This picture does not give the tetrahedral 109.5°; hybridisation is needed to explain it.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-012","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":12,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Use the average bond lengths C–O = 143 pm and C=O = 121 pm. Which option correctly gives the average C–O bond order in $CO_3^{2-}$ and the expected C–O bond length?","image":"","options":["Bond order $\\frac{3}{2}$; all three bonds equal to 121 pm","Bond order $\\frac{4}{3}$; all three bonds equal, lying between 121 pm and 143 pm","Bond order $\\frac{4}{3}$; one bond of 121 pm and two bonds of 143 pm","Bond order 2; all three bonds shorter than 121 pm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Each canonical form has 4 bonds (one double bond and two single bonds) spread over 3 C–O links.\nIn the hybrid these are shared equally, so the average bond order is $\\frac{4}{3} \\approx 1.33$.\nA bond order between 1 and 2 gives a length between that of C=O (121 pm) and C–O (143 pm).\nAll three bonds are equal, so option (3) describes a single canonical form, not the real ion.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-013","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":13,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"What was Langmuir's contribution to the Lewis picture of chemical bonding?","image":"","options":["He proposed that the eight outer electrons occupy the corners of a cube around the kernel","He showed that the stability of ionic solids comes from lattice enthalpy","He gave up the idea of a stationary cubical octet and introduced the term covalent bond","He introduced formal charges to choose the best Lewis structure"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Langmuir refined Lewis's postulates in two ways.\nHe abandoned the idea of a stationary cubical arrangement of the octet.\nHe introduced the term 'covalent bond'.\nOption (1) is tempting, but the cubical octet was Lewis's own idea, which is the very idea Langmuir dropped.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-014","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":14,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"For the $Li_2$ molecule, the number of electrons in bonding molecular orbitals and its bond order are respectively:","image":"","options":["4 and 1","6 and 3","4 and 2","2 and 1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"$Li_2$ has 6 electrons: $(\\sigma 1s)^2(\\sigma^* 1s)^2(\\sigma 2s)^2$.\nBonding electrons: $\\sigma 1s$ (2) + $\\sigma 2s$ (2) = 4. Antibonding electrons: $\\sigma^* 1s$ (2).\nBond order = $\\frac{1}{2}(4-2) = 1$. So $Li_2$ is stable and diamagnetic, and it exists in the vapour phase.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-015","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":15,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following molecules has a zero dipole moment even though each of its bonds is polar?","image":"","options":["$H_2O$","$NH_3$","$BeF_2$","HF"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A molecule's dipole moment is the vector sum of its bond dipoles.\n$BeF_2$ is linear, so its two equal Be–F bond dipoles point in opposite directions and cancel. The net $\\mu$ is 0.\n$H_2O$ is bent, so its O–H bond dipoles do not cancel ($\\mu$ = 1.85 D).\n$NH_3$ is pyramidal and has a net dipole moment.\nHF is a polar diatomic molecule.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-016","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":16,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Taking z as the internuclear axis, match List-I (molecular orbital) with List-II (feature).\nList-I:\n(A) $\\sigma 2p_z$\n(B) $\\sigma^* 2p_z$\n(C) $\\pi 2p_x$\n(D) $\\pi^* 2p_x$\nList-II:\n(i) Not symmetrical about the bond axis; larger electron density above and below the axis\n(ii) Symmetrical about the bond axis; has a nodal plane between the nuclei\n(iii) Not symmetrical about the bond axis; has a node between the nuclei\n(iv) Symmetrical about the bond axis; electron density concentrated between the nuclei","image":"","options":["A-ii, B-iv, C-iii, D-i","A-iv, B-ii, C-i, D-iii","A-iv, B-ii, C-iii, D-i","A-i, B-iii, C-iv, D-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A-iv: $\\sigma 2p_z$ is symmetrical about the bond axis and has electron density between the nuclei, as expected for a bonding MO.\nB-ii: $\\sigma^* 2p_z$ is also axis-symmetric, but it has a nodal plane with zero electron density between the nuclei.\nC-i: $\\pi 2p_x$ is not axis-symmetric; its electron density lies above and below the internuclear axis.\nD-iii: $\\pi^* 2p_x$ is not axis-symmetric and has a node between the nuclei.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-017","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":17,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the Lewis structure of carbon monoxide, $:C\\equiv O:$\nStatement I: The formal charge on carbon is −1 and that on oxygen is +1.\nStatement II: Since formal charges are non-zero, carbon actually carries a full negative charge and oxygen a full positive charge in the molecule.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I: C has 4 valence electrons, 2 non-bonding electrons and 6 bonding electrons, so FC = 4 − 2 − 3 = −1. O has 6 − 2 − 3 = +1. Correct.\nStatement II: NCERT stresses that formal charges do not indicate real charge separation. They only keep track of valence electrons on a purely covalent model. Incorrect.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-018","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":18,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (molecule) with List-II (shape):\nList-I\nA. $BeCl_2$\nB. $CH_4$\nC. $PCl_5$\nD. $SF_6$\nList-II\n(i) Octahedral\n(ii) Linear\n(iii) Trigonal bipyramidal\n(iv) Tetrahedral","image":"","options":["A-ii, B-iii, C-iv, D-i","A-iii, B-iv, C-ii, D-i","A-ii, B-iv, C-i, D-iii","A-ii, B-iv, C-iii, D-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"None of these central atoms carries a lone pair, so each shape follows directly from the number of bond pairs.\nA. $BeCl_2$ has 2 bond pairs, giving a linear shape (ii).\nB. $CH_4$ has 4 bond pairs, giving a tetrahedral shape (iv).\nC. $PCl_5$ has 5 bond pairs, giving a trigonal bipyramidal shape (iii).\nD. $SF_6$ has 6 bond pairs, giving an octahedral shape (i).\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-019","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":19,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A bonding molecular orbital always has lower energy than either of the atomic orbitals that combine to form it.\nReason (R): In a bonding molecular orbital, the electron density is concentrated between the nuclei, so the electrons hold the nuclei together and the repulsion between the nuclei is very small.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true. Constructive interference places electron density between the nuclei, which lowers internuclear repulsion and binds the nuclei together.\nA is true. NCERT states that, because of this stabilising effect, a bonding MO always lies lower in energy than the parent AOs.\nR is therefore the direct reason for A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-020","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":20,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"The central Xe atom in $XeF_4$ has four bond pairs and two lone pairs. Which option correctly describes the arrangement of electron pairs and the shape of the molecule?","image":"","options":["Trigonal bipyramidal arrangement; see-saw shape","Octahedral arrangement; square pyramidal shape","Octahedral arrangement; tetrahedral shape","Octahedral arrangement; square planar shape with the two lone pairs opposite each other"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Xe has 4 + 2 = 6 electron pairs, so the pairs are arranged octahedrally.\nPlacing the two lone pairs trans (180° apart) avoids lp–lp repulsion at 90°, which is the strongest kind of repulsion.\nThe four F atoms then lie in one plane, so the shape is square planar.\nSquare pyramidal is the shape for 5 bp + 1 lp, as in $BrF_5$, so option (2) does not fit.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-021","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":21,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Molecular orbital theory successfully explains the paramagnetic nature of $O_2$. The paramagnetism of $O_2$ arises because:","image":"","options":["two unpaired electrons are present in the $\\sigma 2p_z$ bonding orbital","one electron each occupies the $\\pi^* 2p_x$ and $\\pi^* 2p_y$ orbitals","the $\\sigma^* 2p_z$ orbital contains one unpaired electron","all its molecular orbitals are doubly occupied"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The configuration of $O_2$ (16 electrons) ends in $(\\pi 2p_x)^2(\\pi 2p_y)^2(\\pi^* 2p_x)^1(\\pi^* 2p_y)^1$.\nBy Hund's rule, the two degenerate $\\pi^*$ orbitals are each singly occupied. This gives two unpaired electrons, so $O_2$ is paramagnetic.\nOption (1) is wrong because $\\sigma 2p_z$ is fully paired. Option (3) is wrong because $\\sigma^* 2p_z$ is empty. Option (4) describes a diamagnetic molecule.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-022","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":22,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Covalent radius is half the distance between two similar atoms joined by a covalent bond in the same molecule.\n(ii) For a given atom, the van der Waals radius is smaller than its covalent radius.\n(iii) Bond lengths are measured by spectroscopic, X-ray diffraction and electron diffraction techniques.\n(iv) Mean bond enthalpy is obtained by dividing the total bond dissociation enthalpy by the number of bonds broken.\n(v) In $H_2O$, the energy needed to break the first O–H bond equals that needed to break the second.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct. This is the NCERT definition of covalent radius.\n(ii) Incorrect. The van der Waals radius describes the non-bonded size of the atom, so it is larger. For Cl it is 180 pm versus a covalent radius of 99 pm.\n(iii) Correct. These are the methods NCERT lists.\n(iv) Correct. This is how mean bond enthalpy is defined.\n(v) Incorrect. The values are 502 and 427 kJ $mol^{-1}$ because the chemical environment changes after the first bond breaks.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-023","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":23,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (molecule) with List-II (dipole moment).\nList-I: (A) $H_2O$ (B) $NH_3$ (C) $NF_3$ (D) $CHCl_3$\nList-II: (i) 0.23 D (ii) 1.04 D (iii) 1.85 D (iv) 1.47 D","image":"","options":["A-iii, B-iv, C-i, D-ii","A-iv, B-iii, C-i, D-ii","A-iii, B-i, C-iv, D-ii","A-ii, B-iv, C-i, D-iii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"These values are taken from NCERT Table 4.5.\n(A) $H_2O$, bent: 1.85 D, so iii.\n(B) $NH_3$: 1.47 D, so iv, because its lone-pair dipole adds to the bond moments.\n(C) $NF_3$: 0.23 D, so i, because its lone-pair dipole opposes the bond moments.\n(D) $CHCl_3$, an unsymmetrical tetrahedron: 1.04 D, so ii.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-024","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":24,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"According to Fajans rules, which of the following ionic compounds is expected to have the greatest covalent character?","image":"","options":["KF","NaCl","LiI","KCl"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Fajans rules: covalent character increases as the cation gets smaller and the anion gets larger.\n$Li^+$ is the smallest cation in the list, so it has high polarising power.\n$I^-$ is the largest anion, so it is the most easily polarised.\nLiI therefore has the most covalent character.\nKF combines a large cation with a small anion, so it is the most ionic.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-025","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":25,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"An ionic bond is formed most easily between an element having:","image":"","options":["high ionization enthalpy and an element having a highly negative electron gain enthalpy","low ionization enthalpy and an element having a highly negative electron gain enthalpy","low ionization enthalpy and an element having a positive electron gain enthalpy","high ionization enthalpy and an element having a positive electron gain enthalpy"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"An ionic bond needs a cation and an anion to form easily.\nA low ionization enthalpy means the atom loses an electron readily and forms a cation.\nA highly negative electron gain enthalpy means the atom gains an electron readily and forms an anion.\nOption (1) fails because a high ionization enthalpy makes cation formation difficult.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-026","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":26,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The dipole moment of $NH_3$ (1.47 D) is greater than that of $NF_3$ (0.23 D), even though fluorine is more electronegative than nitrogen.\nReason (R): In $NH_3$, the orbital dipole due to the lone pair on nitrogen acts in the same direction as the resultant of the N–H bond dipoles, whereas in $NF_3$ it opposes the resultant of the N–F bond dipoles.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT gives $\\mu(NH_3)$ = 1.47 D and $\\mu(NF_3)$ = 0.23 D. Both molecules are pyramidal and each has one lone pair on N.\nR is true: in $NH_3$, N is the negative end of the bonds, so the bond moments point the same way as the lone-pair moment and add up. In $NF_3$, the F atoms are the negative ends, so the bond moments oppose the lone-pair moment and partly cancel.\nThis is exactly why $NH_3$ has the larger dipole moment, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-027","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":27,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): $PCl_5$ is a reactive molecule.\nReason (R): In $PCl_5$, the two axial P–Cl bonds experience greater repulsion from the equatorial bond pairs, so they are slightly longer and weaker than the three equatorial bonds.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In $PCl_5$, phosphorus is $sp^3d$ hybridised and the molecule is trigonal bipyramidal.\nThe three equatorial bonds lie in one plane at $120^\\circ$ to each other. The two axial bonds are at $90^\\circ$ to that plane.\nEach axial bond pair is at $90^\\circ$ to three equatorial bond pairs, so it suffers more repulsion. As a result, the axial bonds are slightly longer and weaker.\nNCERT states that these weaker axial bonds make $PCl_5$ more reactive. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-028","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":28,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"In solid chlorine, the internuclear distance between the two Cl atoms within a $Cl_2$ molecule is 198 pm. The closest distance between Cl atoms of neighbouring molecules is 360 pm. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The covalent radius of chlorine is 99 pm.\n(ii) The van der Waals radius of chlorine is 180 pm.\n(iii) The van der Waals radius of chlorine is smaller than its covalent radius.\n(iv) The covalent radius is half the distance between two similar atoms in separate molecules in the solid.\n(v) For a covalent molecule AB, bond length $R = r_A + r_B$, so for $Cl_2$ it equals twice the covalent radius.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: covalent radius = $\\frac{198}{2}$ = 99 pm.\n(ii) Correct: van der Waals radius = $\\frac{360}{2}$ = 180 pm.\n(iii) Incorrect: the van der Waals radius (180 pm) is larger, because it represents the overall size of the nonbonded atom.\n(iv) Incorrect: half the distance between atoms in separate molecules defines the van der Waals radius, not the covalent radius.\n(v) Correct: $R = r_A + r_B = 2r_c$ for $Cl_2$.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-029","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":29,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"How is resonance between canonical structures represented?","image":"","options":["By a pair of half-arrows pointing in opposite directions","By a double-headed arrow between the structures","By a single-headed arrow from one structure to the other","By a dotted line joining the structures"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that resonance is represented by a double-headed arrow (↔) placed between the canonical forms.\nEquilibrium half-arrows would wrongly suggest that the forms interconvert in an equilibrium.\nA single arrow is used for a reaction, and a dotted line is used for a hydrogen bond.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-030","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":30,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (molecule) with List-II (shape):\nList-I: (a) $SF_4$ (b) $ClF_3$ (c) $NH_3$ (d) $BF_3$\nList-II: (i) T-shaped (ii) trigonal planar (iii) see-saw (iv) trigonal pyramidal","image":"","options":["a-iii, b-i, c-iv, d-ii","a-i, b-iii, c-iv, d-ii","a-iii, b-i, c-ii, d-iv","a-iv, b-i, c-iii, d-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) $SF_4$: 4 bond pairs + 1 lone pair (AB$_4$E). The lone pair sits in an equatorial position, giving a see-saw shape → (iii).\n(b) $ClF_3$: 3 bond pairs + 2 lone pairs (AB$_3$E$_2$). Both lone pairs are equatorial, giving a T-shape → (i).\n(c) $NH_3$: 3 bond pairs + 1 lone pair → trigonal pyramidal (iv).\n(d) $BF_3$: 3 bond pairs, no lone pair → trigonal planar (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-031","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":31,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The bond length in $N_2$ (109 pm) is shorter than that in $O_2$ (121 pm).\nReason (R): The bond order of $N_2$ is less than that of $O_2$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT lists the N≡N bond length as 109 pm and the O=O bond length as 121 pm.\nR is false: $N_2$ has a triple bond (bond order 3), while $O_2$ has a double bond (bond order 2).\nA higher bond order gives a shorter, stronger bond. This is why $N_2$ has the shorter bond length and the higher bond enthalpy (946 kJ mol$^{-1}$ versus 498 kJ mol$^{-1}$).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-032","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":32,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"According to molecular orbital theory, how many of the following species are paramagnetic?\n$O_2$, $B_2$, $C_2$, $N_2$, $Li_2$, $O_2^-$, $O_2^{2-}$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"$O_2$ has two unpaired electrons in $\\pi^*2p_x$ and $\\pi^*2p_y$, so it is paramagnetic.\n$B_2$ has two unpaired electrons in $\\pi2p_x$ and $\\pi2p_y$, so it is paramagnetic.\n$O_2^-$ has one unpaired $\\pi^*$ electron, so it is paramagnetic.\n$C_2$, $N_2$, $Li_2$ and $O_2^{2-}$ have all electrons paired, so they are diamagnetic.\nTotal paramagnetic = 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-033","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":33,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (species) with List-II (bond order and magnetic behaviour) according to molecular orbital theory.\nList-I:\n(A) $O_2$\n(B) $O_2^-$\n(C) $O_2^{2-}$\n(D) $C_2$\nList-II:\n(i) Bond order 1.5, paramagnetic\n(ii) Bond order 2, paramagnetic\n(iii) Bond order 2, diamagnetic\n(iv) Bond order 1, diamagnetic","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(i), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(iii)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"$O_2$: 10 bonding and 6 antibonding electrons give bond order 2, with two unpaired $\\pi^*$ electrons → paramagnetic (ii).\n$O_2^-$: 10 and 7 electrons give bond order 1.5, with one unpaired electron → (i).\n$O_2^{2-}$: 10 and 8 electrons give bond order 1, with all electrons paired → (iv).\n$C_2$: 8 and 4 electrons give bond order 2, with the four $\\pi$ electrons paired → diamagnetic (iii).\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-034","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":34,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following molecules have fewer than eight electrons around the central atom?\n$BeH_2$, $BCl_3$, $AlCl_3$, $SF_6$, $PF_5$, $CH_4$, $H_2SO_4$, $NH_3$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Incomplete octet (central atom has fewer than four valence electrons): $BeH_2$ has 4 electrons around Be, $BCl_3$ has 6 around B and $AlCl_3$ has 6 around Al.\nExpanded octet: $SF_6$, $PF_5$ and $H_2SO_4$ have more than eight electrons around the central atom.\nOctet obeyed: $CH_4$ and $NH_3$.\nTotal with incomplete octet = 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-035","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":35,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The H–O–H bond angle in water (104.5°) is smaller than the H–N–H bond angle in ammonia (107°).\nReason (R): The oxygen atom in water carries two lone pairs, and lone pair–lone pair repulsion is stronger than lone pair–bond pair repulsion.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT gives the angles as 104.5° for $H_2O$ and 107° for $NH_3$. Both are reduced from the tetrahedral value of 109.5°.\nR is true: oxygen in water has 2 lp and 2 bp, while nitrogen in ammonia has only 1 lp.\nThe extra lp–lp repulsion in water squeezes the bond pairs closer together than in ammonia. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-036","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":36,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Although forming $Na^+(g)$ and $Cl^-(g)$ from gaseous Na and Cl atoms is an endothermic process overall, solid NaCl is a stable compound.\nReason (R): The energy released in forming the crystal lattice of NaCl is more than the net energy absorbed in forming the gaseous ions.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Ionization enthalpy plus electron gain enthalpy = 495.8 − 348.7 = +147.1 kJ mol$^{-1}$, which is energy absorbed, yet NaCl(s) is stable.\nR is true. The enthalpy of lattice formation is −788 kJ mol$^{-1}$, which more than compensates for the 147.1 kJ mol$^{-1}$ absorbed.\nR therefore explains A directly: overall, more energy is released than absorbed.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-037","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":37,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"For the Lewis structure of the nitrite ion, NO$_2^-$, written with one N=O bond and one N–O bond so that every atom has an octet, which statement is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The total number of valence electrons used is 18","The nitrogen atom carries one lone pair and a formal charge of zero","The singly bonded oxygen carries three lone pairs and a formal charge of −1","The ion contains a total of four lone pairs of electrons"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: 5 + 2 × 6 + 1 = 18 valence electrons.\n(2) Correct: N has 3 bonds and 1 lone pair, so its formal charge = 5 − 2 − 3 = 0.\n(3) Correct: the singly bonded O has formal charge = 6 − 6 − 1 = −1.\n(4) Incorrect: count the lone pairs. N has 1, the double-bonded O has 2 and the single-bonded O has 3, giving 6 lone pairs, not 4.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-038","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":38,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"In the methane molecule, carbon uses $sp^3$ hybrid orbitals to bond with hydrogen. What is the percentage p-character in each of these hybrid orbitals?","image":"","options":["25%","50%","66.7%","75%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Key fact: one s orbital and three p orbitals mix to form four equivalent $sp^3$ hybrid orbitals.\nEach hybrid therefore has $\\frac{1}{4}$ = 25% s-character and $\\frac{3}{4}$ = 75% p-character.\n25% is the tempting wrong choice, but it is the s-character, not the p-character.\n50% is the s-character of an sp hybrid.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-039","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":39,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Arrange the following molecules in DECREASING order of the bond angle at the central atom:\n$CH_4$, $SO_2$, $NH_3$, $BF_3$, $H_2O$","image":"","options":["$SO_2$ > $BF_3$ > $CH_4$ > $NH_3$ > $H_2O$","$BF_3$ > $SO_2$ > $CH_4$ > $NH_3$ > $H_2O$","$BF_3$ > $CH_4$ > $SO_2$ > $NH_3$ > $H_2O$","$BF_3$ > $SO_2$ > $CH_4$ > $H_2O$ > $NH_3$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"$BF_3$ is trigonal planar with no lone pair: $120^\\circ$.\n$SO_2$ has a trigonal planar arrangement with one lone pair, which cuts the angle slightly to $119.5^\\circ$.\n$CH_4$ is a regular tetrahedron: $109.5^\\circ$.\n$NH_3$ has a tetrahedral arrangement with one lone pair: $107^\\circ$.\n$H_2O$ has two lone pairs, adding lp–lp repulsion: $104.5^\\circ$.\nThe order is $BF_3$ > $SO_2$ > $CH_4$ > $NH_3$ > $H_2O$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-040","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":40,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, the lattice enthalpy of an ionic solid is defined as the energy:","image":"","options":["released when one mole of the solid ionic compound is formed from its neutral gaseous atoms","required to completely separate one mole of the solid ionic compound into its gaseous constituent ions","required to convert one mole of the solid ionic compound into its constituent elements in their standard states","released when one mole of gaseous atoms of the metal loses electrons"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT defines lattice enthalpy as the energy required to completely separate one mole of a solid ionic compound into its gaseous constituent ions.\nFor NaCl this is the change NaCl(s) → Na$^+$(g) + Cl$^-$(g).\nOption (1) is wrong because it starts from neutral atoms, not ions, and it describes energy released rather than energy required.\nOption (3) describes decomposition into elements, which is a different quantity.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-041","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":41,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): $BeF_2$ has zero dipole moment even though each Be–F bond is polar.\nReason (R): In $BeF_2$ the two equal bond dipoles point in opposite directions and cancel each other.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: $BeF_2$ is linear and its net dipole moment is zero.\nR is true: the two equal Be–F bond dipoles are oriented at 180° to each other, so their vector sum is zero.\nThis cancellation is exactly why the net moment vanishes, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-042","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":42,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"According to MO theory, the double bond in the $C_2$ molecule consists of:","image":"","options":["one sigma bond and one pi bond","two sigma bonds","one sigma bond and one half pi bond","two pi bonds"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"$C_2$ has 12 electrons: $KK(\\sigma 2s)^2(\\sigma^* 2s)^2(\\pi 2p_x)^2(\\pi 2p_y)^2$.\nThe four bonding electrons beyond the 2s pair are all in the two $\\pi$ MOs. The $\\sigma 2p_z$ orbital is empty.\nSo the bond order is 2, and both bonds are pi bonds. This is unlike most molecules, where a double bond is one sigma plus one pi bond.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-043","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":43,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In a chain of hydrogen fluoride molecules, a hydrogen atom acts as a bridge between two fluorine atoms.\nReason (R): The hydrogen atom is held to one fluorine atom by a covalent bond and to the other fluorine atom by a hydrogen bond.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT describes the hydrogen in an HF chain as a bridge between two F atoms.\nR is true: that H is covalently bonded to the F of its own molecule. It is attracted by a hydrogen bond to the F of the neighbouring molecule.\nR explains A, because the bridge exists only because one H is bonded to two F atoms in these two different ways.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-044","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":44,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) O–O bond in $O_3$ (experimental)\n(b) C–O bond in $CO_2$ (experimental)\n(c) Normal O–O single bond\n(d) Normal C=O double bond\nList-II\n(i) 148 pm\n(ii) 121 pm\n(iii) 128 pm\n(iv) 115 pm","image":"","options":["a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii","a-ii, b-iv, c-i, d-iii","a-iii, b-ii, c-i, d-iv","a-i, b-iv, c-iii, d-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) In $O_3$ both O–O bonds are 128 pm, which lies between a single and a double bond → (iii).\n(b) In $CO_2$ the C–O bond is 115 pm, which lies between C=O (121) and C≡O (110) → (iv).\n(c) A normal O–O single bond is 148 pm → (i).\n(d) A normal C=O bond is 121 pm → (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-045","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":45,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The ionization of a gaseous atom is always endothermic.\n(ii) The gain of an electron by a gaseous atom is always exothermic.\n(iii) Electron affinity is the negative of the energy change accompanying electron gain.\n(iv) The lattice enthalpy of an ionic solid can be calculated directly from the forces of attraction and repulsion between ions alone.\n(v) The crystal structure of an ionic compound depends on the size of the ions and their packing arrangement.\n(vi) Separating an ionic solid into gaseous ions involves both attraction between oppositely charged ions and repulsion between like-charged ions.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: ionization is always endothermic.\n(ii) Incorrect: electron gain may be exothermic or endothermic.\n(iii) Correct: this is how NCERT defines electron affinity.\n(iv) Incorrect: the crystal is three-dimensional, so factors from the crystal geometry must also be included.\n(v) Correct: crystal structure depends on ion size, packing arrangement and other factors.\n(vi) Correct: NCERT says both kinds of force are involved.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-046","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":46,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the carbon–oxygen bonds in carbon monoxide (CO), carbon dioxide ($CO_2$) and the carbonate ion ($CO_3^{2-}$). Which order of carbon–oxygen bond length is correct?","image":"","options":["$CO_2$ < CO < $CO_3^{2-}$","CO < $CO_2$ < $CO_3^{2-}$","$CO_3^{2-}$ < $CO_2$ < CO","CO < $CO_3^{2-}$ < $CO_2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"CO is isoelectronic with $N_2$, so its bond order is 3. This gives the shortest bond, about 110 pm.\n$CO_2$ is a resonance hybrid. NCERT gives its C–O length as 115 pm, between C≡O (110 pm) and C=O (121 pm), so its bond order lies between 2 and 3.\n$CO_3^{2-}$ is a hybrid of three forms, each with one C=O and two C–O bonds. Its bond order is 4/3, between a single and a double bond, so its bond is the longest.\nBond length decreases as bond order increases, giving CO < $CO_2$ < $CO_3^{2-}$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-047","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":47,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"In the formation of $CaF_2$ by electron transfer, the electrovalences of calcium and fluorine are respectively:","image":"","options":["+1 and −2","+2 and −2","+2 and −1","+1 and −1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Electrovalence equals the number of unit charges on the ion.\nCa ([Ar]$4s^2$) loses 2 electrons to form $Ca^{2+}$, so its electrovalence is +2.\nEach F ($2s^22p^5$) gains 1 electron to form $F^-$, so its electrovalence is −1.\nTwo $F^-$ ions balance one $Ca^{2+}$, which gives the formula $CaF_2$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-048","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":48,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about molecular orbital theory is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The number of molecular orbitals formed equals the number of atomic orbitals combined","In an antibonding molecular orbital, most of the electron density is concentrated in the region between the two nuclei","A bonding molecular orbital is lower in energy than either of the atomic orbitals that combine to form it","For $O_2$ and $F_2$, the $\\sigma 2p_z$ orbital lies lower in energy than the $\\pi 2p_x$ and $\\pi 2p_y$ orbitals"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: when two atomic orbitals combine, two molecular orbitals are formed.\n(2) Incorrect: an antibonding MO arises from destructive interference. Its electron density lies away from the internuclear region, and it has a nodal plane between the nuclei.\n(3) Correct: constructive interference lowers the energy of the bonding MO below both parent atomic orbitals.\n(4) Correct: for $O_2$ and $F_2$, $\\sigma 2p_z$ lies below $\\pi 2p$. The order is reversed for $B_2$, $C_2$ and $N_2$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-049","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":49,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In $PCl_5$, the axial P–Cl bonds are slightly longer than the equatorial P–Cl bonds.\nReason (R): The axial bond pairs suffer more repulsive interaction from the equatorial bond pairs.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: in trigonal bipyramidal $PCl_5$, the two axial bonds are longer and weaker than the three equatorial bonds. This is why $PCl_5$ is reactive.\nR is true: each axial bond pair makes 90° with three equatorial bond pairs, so it is repelled more strongly.\nThis greater repulsion is exactly what lengthens the axial bonds, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-050","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":50,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the conditions for hybridisation is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Only orbitals present in the valence shell of the atom are hybridised.","The orbitals undergoing hybridisation should have almost equal energy.","Promotion of an electron to a higher orbital is an essential condition before hybridisation can occur.","Even completely filled orbitals of the valence shell can take part in hybridisation."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: hybridisation involves valence-shell orbitals only.\n(2) Correct: the orbitals that mix must be close in energy.\n(3) Incorrect: NCERT states that promotion of an electron is not an essential condition. In $NH_3$, for example, N forms $sp^3$ hybrids without any promotion.\n(4) Correct: filled orbitals can also hybridise. The lone-pair orbitals in $NH_3$ and $H_2O$ are $sp^3$ hybrids.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-051","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":51,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The bond dissociation enthalpy of $N_2$ (946 kJ $mol^{-1}$) is much larger than that of $O_2$ (498 kJ $mol^{-1}$).\nReason (R): The bond order in $N_2$ is 3 whereas that in $O_2$ is 2, and bond enthalpy increases with increase in bond order.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT gives $\\Delta_aH$ = 946.0 kJ $mol^{-1}$ for $N_2$ and 498 kJ $mol^{-1}$ for $O_2$.\nR is true. $N\\equiv N$ shares three electron pairs (bond order 3), while O=O shares two (bond order 2).\nAs bond order increases, bond enthalpy increases and bond length decreases. This general correlation directly accounts for the larger value for $N_2$, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-052","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":52,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In PF$_5$, the phosphorus atom has more than eight electrons in its valence shell.\nReason (R): Elements of the third period and beyond can use d orbitals, in addition to s and p orbitals, for bonding.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. P forms five P–F bonds, so 10 electrons surround the phosphorus atom (an expanded octet).\nR is true. Third-period elements have d orbitals available for bonding, besides s and p orbitals.\nThe availability of these d orbitals is exactly what lets P hold more than eight electrons, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-053","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":53,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"$C_2$ is observed experimentally to be diamagnetic. Suppose, hypothetically, that its molecular orbitals followed the energy order used for $O_2$ and $F_2$, with $\\sigma 2p_z$ below $\\pi 2p_x$ and $\\pi 2p_y$. Which prediction would then follow for $C_2$?","image":"","options":["Bond order 2 and diamagnetic","Bond order 3 and diamagnetic","Bond order 1 and paramagnetic","Bond order 2 and paramagnetic"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"$C_2$ has 12 electrons.\nWith the $O_2$-type order, the configuration is $KK\\,(\\sigma 2s)^2(\\sigma^* 2s)^2(\\sigma 2p_z)^2(\\pi 2p_x)^1(\\pi 2p_y)^1$, because Hund's rule puts one electron in each degenerate $\\pi$ MO.\nCounting electrons: $N_b = 8$ and $N_a = 4$, so the bond order is $\\frac{8-4}{2} = 2$.\nThe two unpaired electrons would make $C_2$ paramagnetic.\nThe actual order is $(\\pi 2p)^4$ below $\\sigma 2p_z$, which pairs all electrons and explains the observed diamagnetism.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-054","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":54,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements regarding molecular shapes predicted by VSEPR theory is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["In $ClF_3$, both lone pairs occupy equatorial positions, so the molecule is T-shaped.","In $XeF_4$, the two lone pairs lie opposite each other, so the four F atoms are square planar.","In $BrF_5$, all F–Br–F bond angles are exactly $90^\\circ$, because the lone pair does not affect the bond angles.","In $PCl_5$, the bond angles are not all equal; some are $120^\\circ$ and others are $90^\\circ$."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct. Equatorial lone pairs minimise the lp–bp repulsions at $90^\\circ$, giving a T-shaped $ClF_3$.\n(2) Correct. Lone pairs placed trans to each other keep the lp–lp repulsion lowest, giving a square planar $XeF_4$.\n(3) Incorrect. The lone pair in $BrF_5$ repels the bond pairs more strongly than they repel each other, so the angles deviate from the ideal $90^\\circ$. Lone pairs always distort idealised angles.\n(4) Correct. In $PCl_5$, equatorial–equatorial angles are $120^\\circ$ and axial–equatorial angles are $90^\\circ$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-055","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":55,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"A 1s orbital of one hydrogen-like atom can combine with the 1s orbital of an identical atom, but not with its 2s orbital. The reason is that:","image":"","options":["the energy of the 2s orbital is appreciably higher than that of the 1s orbital","the 1s and 2s orbitals have different symmetry about the molecular axis","the 1s and 2s orbitals cannot overlap at all","the 2s orbital has more electrons than the 1s orbital"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The first condition for combining AOs is that they must have the same or nearly the same energy.\nThe 2s orbital lies appreciably higher in energy than the 1s orbital, so 1s–2s combination is not effective for identical atoms.\nBoth s orbitals are spherically symmetrical, so their symmetry about the axis is the same. Option 2 therefore fails.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-056","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":56,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"According to VSEPR theory, which of the following molecules has a trigonal bipyramidal shape (geometry of the atoms)?","image":"","options":["$BF_3$","$SF_4$","$PCl_5$","$ClF_3$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In $PCl_5$, P has five bond pairs and no lone pair. The five electron pairs, and so the five Cl atoms, take up a trigonal bipyramidal arrangement.\n$SF_4$ and $ClF_3$ also have five electron pairs around the central atom. However, one or two of these are lone pairs, so their shapes are see-saw and T-shaped.\n$BF_3$ has three bond pairs, so it is trigonal planar.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-057","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":57,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"In $SF_4$ (4 bond pairs and 1 lone pair on S), the lone pair occupies an equatorial position of the trigonal bipyramid rather than an axial one. Why?","image":"","options":["A lone pair occupies less space than a bond pair, so it fits better in the crowded equatorial plane.","At an equatorial position the lone pair has only two lone pair–bond pair repulsions at 90°, compared with three at an axial position.","At an equatorial position the lone pair has no 90° interactions with bond pairs at all.","At an axial position the lone pair has three neighbours at 120°, which raises the repulsion."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A lone pair at an axial site is 90° away from all three equatorial pairs, giving three lp–bp repulsions at 90°.\nAt an equatorial site it is 90° away from only the two axial pairs, giving two such repulsions. This is lower in energy, so the lone pair sits equatorially and the shape is see-saw.\nOption (1) fails because a lone pair actually occupies more space than a bond pair.\nOption (3) fails because two 90° interactions still remain.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-058","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":58,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following pairs of species has identical bond orders?","image":"","options":["$N_2$ and $O_2$","$CO$ and $O_2$","$F_2$ and $O_2^{2-}$","$NO^+$ and $O_2^{2-}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Isoelectronic molecules and ions have identical bond orders.\n$F_2$ and $O_2^{2-}$ each have 18 electrons, so both have bond order 1.\n$N_2$, $CO$ and $NO^+$ have bond order 3, while $O_2$ has bond order 2. Every other pair therefore mismatches.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-059","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":59,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Taking the z-axis as the internuclear axis, which pair of half-filled orbitals forms a pi ($\\pi$) bond?","image":"","options":["$1s$ and $1s$","$1s$ and $2p_z$","$2p_z$ and $2p_z$","$2p_x$ and $2p_x$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A $\\pi$ bond forms by sidewise overlap. In it, the orbital axes are parallel to each other and perpendicular to the internuclear axis.\nTwo $2p_x$ orbitals are perpendicular to z and parallel to each other, so they overlap sidewise to give a $\\pi$ bond.\n$1s-1s$, $1s-2p_z$ and $2p_z-2p_z$ overlap head-on along z, so each gives a $\\sigma$ bond.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-060","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":60,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"In VSEPR theory, which option gives the correct decreasing order of repulsive interactions between electron pairs in the valence shell?","image":"","options":["lone pair–lone pair > lone pair–bond pair > bond pair–bond pair","bond pair–bond pair > lone pair–bond pair > lone pair–lone pair","lone pair–bond pair > lone pair–lone pair > bond pair–bond pair","lone pair–lone pair > bond pair–bond pair > lone pair–bond pair"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Nyholm and Gillespie pointed out that a lone pair is localised on the central atom only, so it occupies more space than a bond pair.\nA bond pair is shared between two nuclei and spreads out less around the central atom.\nTherefore repulsions decrease in the order lp–lp > lp–bp > bp–bp.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-061","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":61,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following species is isoelectronic with $F_2$ and therefore has the same bond order as $F_2$?","image":"","options":["$NO^+$","$O_2^{2-}$","$O_2$","$N_2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"$F_2$ has 18 electrons. $O_2^{2-}$ has 16 + 2 = 18 electrons, so it is isoelectronic with $F_2$.\nNCERT states that isoelectronic species have identical bond orders, so both $F_2$ and $O_2^{2-}$ have bond order 1.\n$N_2$ and $NO^+$ have 14 electrons each and bond order 3.\n$O_2$ has 16 electrons and bond order 2.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-062","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":62,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["In $PCl_5$, the axial P–Cl bonds are slightly longer than the equatorial bonds because axial bond pairs suffer greater repulsion from equatorial bond pairs.","According to MO theory, the double bond in $C_2$ consists of two $\\pi$ bonds.","Promotion of an electron is not an essential condition prior to hybridisation.","Because 3d orbitals are comparable in energy to 3s and 3p, hybridisation involving 3p, 3d and 4s orbitals is also possible."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: axial bonds face more repulsion, so they are longer and weaker, which makes $PCl_5$ reactive.\n(2) Correct: in $C_2$, the four bonding electrons occupy the two $\\pi$ MOs, so the double bond is made of two $\\pi$ bonds.\n(3) Correct: NCERT states that promotion is not essential, and even filled orbitals may take part.\n(4) Incorrect: the energy gap between 3p and 4s is significant, so NCERT rules out hybridisation involving 3p, 3d and 4s.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-063","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":63,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In both $SF_6$ and $[Co(NH_3)_6]^{3+}$, the central atom uses six equivalent hybrid orbitals directed towards the corners of a regular octahedron.\nStatement II: In both species, the d orbitals used in hybridisation belong to a lower principal shell than the s orbital used.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true.","Statement I is true but Statement II is false.","Statement I is false but Statement II is true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are false."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is true. $SF_6$ ($sp^3d^2$) and $[Co(NH_3)_6]^{3+}$ ($d^2sp^3$) both have six hybrids arranged octahedrally.\nStatement II is false. In $SF_6$, sulphur uses 3s, 3p and 3d orbitals, so its d orbitals are in the same shell as the s orbital (outer d).\nOnly in $d^2sp^3$ for Co are the d orbitals (3d) from a lower shell than the s orbital (4s).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-064","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":64,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Most ionic compounds have cations derived from metals and anions derived from non-metals. Which ion is cited by NCERT as an exception, because it forms the cation of many ionic compounds even though it is made up only of non-metallic elements?","image":"","options":["$NO_3^-$","$H_3O^+$","$NH_4^+$","$SO_4^{2-}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Cations of ionic compounds are usually metal ions.\nThe ammonium ion, $NH_4^+$, contains only nitrogen and hydrogen, both non-metals. It still acts as the cation in many ionic compounds, such as $NH_4Cl$. NCERT names it as the exception.\n$NO_3^-$ and $SO_4^{2-}$ are anions, so they are not exceptions to this rule.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-065","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":65,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (type of overlap) with List-II (bond in which it occurs):\nList-I\n(A) s–s overlap\n(B) s–p axial overlap\n(C) p–p axial overlap\n(D) p–p sidewise overlap\nList-II\n(i) $\\pi$ bond between carbon atoms in $C_2H_4$\n(ii) H–H bond in $H_2$\n(iii) Cl–Cl bond in $Cl_2$\n(iv) H–Cl bond in HCl","image":"","options":["A-ii, B-iv, C-iii, D-i","A-ii, B-iii, C-iv, D-i","A-iv, B-ii, C-iii, D-i","A-ii, B-iv, C-i, D-iii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) $H_2$ forms from two half-filled 1s orbitals: s–s, so A-ii.\n(B) H–Cl forms from H 1s and Cl 3p head-on: s–p, so B-iv.\n(C) Cl–Cl forms from two 3p orbitals head-on: p–p axial, so C-iii.\n(D) The $\\pi$ bond in ethene comes from unhybridised 2p orbitals overlapping sidewise, so D-i.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-066","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":66,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (process) with List-II (enthalpy change, kJ mol$^{-1}$):\nList-I\n(a) $Na(g) \\rightarrow Na^+(g) + e^-$\n(b) $Cl(g) + e^- \\rightarrow Cl^-(g)$\n(c) $Na^+(g) + Cl^-(g) \\rightarrow NaCl(s)$\n(d) $NaCl(s) \\rightarrow Na^+(g) + Cl^-(g)$\nList-II\n(i) $-348.7$\n(ii) $+788$\n(iii) $+495.8$\n(iv) $-788$","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) This is ionization of Na. Ionization is always endothermic, so the value is +495.8 → (iii).\n(b) This is electron gain by Cl. It is exothermic here, so the value is −348.7 → (i).\n(c) This is lattice formation from gaseous ions. Energy is released, so the value is −788 → (iv).\n(d) This is the lattice enthalpy as defined, separating the solid into gaseous ions. Energy is required, so the value is +788 → (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-067","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":67,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"For water, the enthalpy to break the first O–H bond is 502 kJ $mol^{-1}$ and the second O–H bond is 427 kJ $mol^{-1}$. How much energy is needed to completely atomise 3.6 g of gaseous water into H and O atoms? (Molar mass of $H_2O$ = 18 g $mol^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["371.6 kJ","92.9 kJ","185.8 kJ","464.5 kJ"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: the two O–H bond enthalpies are 502 and 427 kJ $mol^{-1}$, and the mass of water is 3.6 g.\nFormula: energy = moles × total bond dissociation enthalpy per mole.\nMoles of water = 3.6/18 = 0.2 mol.\nTotal enthalpy for atomising 1 mol = 502 + 427 = 929 kJ.\nSubstituting: 0.2 × 929 = 185.8 kJ. Note that 464.5 kJ is only the average O–H bond enthalpy.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-068","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":68,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In $PCl_5$, the axial P–Cl bonds are slightly longer and weaker than the equatorial P–Cl bonds.\nReason (R): Each axial bond pair faces more repulsive interaction from the equatorial bond pairs, which lie at $90^\\circ$ to it.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false.","A is false but R is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. The axial bonds in $PCl_5$ are slightly longer and weaker, which makes the molecule more reactive.\nR is true. Each axial bond is at $90^\\circ$ to the three equatorial bonds, so it suffers more repulsion. The equatorial bonds are $120^\\circ$ apart from each other.\nThis greater repulsion is exactly why the axial bonds lengthen, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-069","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":69,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"The dipole moment of water is 1.85 D. What is its value in SI units? (Take 1 D = $3.336 \\times 10^{-30}$ C m)","image":"","options":["$6.17 \\times 10^{-30}$ C m","$0.55 \\times 10^{-30}$ C m","$18.5 \\times 10^{-30}$ C m","$1.85 \\times 10^{-30}$ C m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\mu$ = 1.85 D and 1 D = $3.336 \\times 10^{-30}$ C m.\nFormula: $\\mu$ (C m) = $\\mu$ (D) $\\times 3.336 \\times 10^{-30}$.\nSubstituting: $1.85 \\times 3.336 \\times 10^{-30} = 6.17 \\times 10^{-30}$ C m.\nThe value $0.55 \\times 10^{-30}$ C m comes from dividing by the conversion factor instead of multiplying.\nSo $\\mu = 6.17 \\times 10^{-30}$ C m.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-070","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":70,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (species) with List-II (hybridisation of the central atom):\nList-I\n(A) $[Ni(CN)_4]^{2-}$\n(B) $PCl_5$\n(C) $SF_6$\n(D) $[Co(NH_3)_6]^{3+}$\nList-II\n(I) $sp^3d$\n(II) $d^2sp^3$\n(III) $dsp^2$\n(IV) $sp^3d^2$","image":"","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) $[Ni(CN)_4]^{2-}$ is square planar and uses $d+s+p(2)$, which is $dsp^2$ (III).\n(B) $PCl_5$ is trigonal bipyramidal and uses $s+p(3)+d$, which is $sp^3d$ (I).\n(C) $SF_6$ is octahedral and uses outer d orbitals, $s+p(3)+d(2)$, which is $sp^3d^2$ (IV).\n(D) $[Co(NH_3)_6]^{3+}$ is octahedral and uses inner d orbitals, $d(2)+s+p(3)$, which is $d^2sp^3$ (II).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-071","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":71,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (species) with List-II (bond order):\nList-I\nA. $O_2^+$\nB. $O_2^-$\nC. $O_2^{2-}$\nD. $N_2$\nList-II\n(i) 1\n(ii) 1.5\n(iii) 2.5\n(iv) 3","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"$O_2$ has $N_b = 10$ and $N_a = 6$, so its bond order is 2.\nA. $O_2^+$ loses one $\\pi^*$ electron: $\\frac{1}{2}(10-5) = 2.5$, matching (iii).\nB. $O_2^-$ gains one $\\pi^*$ electron: $\\frac{1}{2}(10-7) = 1.5$, matching (ii).\nC. $O_2^{2-}$ gains two $\\pi^*$ electrons: $\\frac{1}{2}(10-8) = 1$, matching (i).\nD. $N_2$: $\\frac{1}{2}(10-4) = 3$, matching (iv).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-072","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":72,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (molecule) with List-II (octet status of the central atom).\nList-I:\n(a) BCl$_3$\n(b) NO$_2$\n(c) SF$_6$\n(d) SCl$_2$\nList-II:\n(i) Expanded octet\n(ii) Octet obeyed\n(iii) Incomplete octet\n(iv) Odd-electron molecule","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) BCl$_3$: boron has only 6 electrons around it, so the octet is incomplete → (iii).\n(b) NO$_2$: it has 17 valence electrons, so it is an odd-electron molecule → (iv).\n(c) SF$_6$: sulphur has 12 electrons around it, an expanded octet → (i).\n(d) SCl$_2$: sulphur has exactly an octet, showing that S can also obey the rule → (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-073","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":73,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Bond angle is the angle between the orbitals containing bonding electron pairs around the central atom in a molecule or complex ion, and it can be determined experimentally by spectroscopic methods.\nStatement II: The H–O–H bond angle in the water molecule is 104.5°.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. This is the NCERT definition of bond angle, and the angle is measured spectroscopically. It indicates how orbitals are distributed around the central atom and so helps fix the shape.\nStatement II is correct. The H–O–H angle in water is 104.5°. It is not the tetrahedral 109.5° or 90°.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-074","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":74,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Aluminium chloride reacts with a chloride ion as follows:\n$AlCl_3 + Cl^- \\rightarrow AlCl_4^-$\nWhat change, if any, occurs in the hybridisation of the Al atom?","image":"","options":["$sp^2$ to $sp^3$","$sp^3$ to $sp^2$","$sp^2$ to $dsp^2$","No change; Al remains $sp^3$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In $AlCl_3$, Al forms three bonds and has no lone pair. It is $sp^2$ hybridised and trigonal planar, like $BCl_3$.\nIn $AlCl_4^-$, Al forms four bonds after accepting an electron pair from $Cl^-$. It becomes $sp^3$ hybridised and tetrahedral.\nThe hybridisation therefore changes from $sp^2$ to $sp^3$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-075","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":75,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the Lewis structure of carbon monoxide in which C and O are joined by a triple bond.\nStatement I: Both carbon and oxygen carry one lone pair each, and both atoms complete their octets.\nStatement II: The formal charge on carbon in this structure is +1.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"CO has 4 + 6 = 10 valence electrons. The triple bond uses 6 of them, leaving 2 lone pairs, one on each atom.\nEach atom then has 6 bonding electrons + 2 lone-pair electrons = 8, so Statement I is correct.\nFormal charge on C = 4 − 2 − ½ × 6 = −1, and on O = 6 − 2 − 3 = +1.\nSo carbon is −1, not +1, and Statement II is incorrect.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-076","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":76,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The carbonate ion does not spend a fraction of time in one canonical form and the rest of the time in the other forms.\nReason (R): The canonical forms of a species are in dynamic equilibrium with one another, just like keto and enol forms in tautomerism.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. The ion has a single real structure, the resonance hybrid, and it does not flip between forms.\nR is false. NCERT states clearly that there is no equilibrium between canonical forms like the one between keto and enol tautomers.\nTautomers are real, separate species. Canonical forms have no real existence.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-077","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":77,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The carbon–oxygen bond in $CO_2$ (115 pm) is shorter than a normal C=O double bond (121 pm).\nStatement II: $CO_2$ is best described as a resonance hybrid of canonical forms, some of which contain a C≡O triple bond.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The experimental C–O length in $CO_2$ is 115 pm, which is less than 121 pm.\nThis length lies between C=O (121 pm) and C≡O (110 pm), so no single Lewis structure fits it.\nStatement II is correct. NCERT describes $CO_2$ as a hybrid of three canonical forms. Besides O=C=O, these include forms with one C≡O and one C–O bond.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-078","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":78,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following molecules have at least one lone pair on the central atom?\n$BeCl_2$, $BF_3$, $SO_2$, $NH_3$, $CH_4$, $SF_4$, $PCl_5$, $XeF_4$, $SF_6$","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"These have no lone pair on the central atom: $BeCl_2$ (AB2), $BF_3$ (AB3), $CH_4$ (AB4), $PCl_5$ (AB5) and $SF_6$ (AB6).\nThese have lone pairs on the central atom:\n$SO_2$ (1 lone pair)\n$NH_3$ (1 lone pair)\n$SF_4$ (1 lone pair)\n$XeF_4$ (2 lone pairs)\nSo the count is 4.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-079","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":79,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"In the representation of hydrogen fluoride\n$\\delta^+$H–F$^{\\delta-}$ ···· $\\delta^+$H–F$^{\\delta-}$ ···· $\\delta^+$H–F$^{\\delta-}$\nwhat does the dotted line stand for?","image":"","options":["A covalent bond between H and F of the same molecule","A hydrogen bond between H of one molecule and F of another molecule","An ionic bond between H$^+$ and F$^-$ ions","A coordinate bond donated by H to F"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In NCERT's convention, a solid line represents a covalent bond and a dotted line represents a hydrogen bond.\nThe dotted line joins the $\\delta^+$ H of one HF molecule to the $\\delta^-$ F of the next molecule. That is an intermolecular hydrogen bond.\nOption (1) is wrong because the H–F bond within one molecule is the covalent bond, shown by the solid line.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-080","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":80,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is an odd-electron molecule, in which the octet rule cannot be satisfied for all the atoms?","image":"","options":["NO$_2$","BCl$_3$","SF$_6$","CO$_2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Count the valence electrons. NO$_2$ has 5 + 2 × 6 = 17, an odd number, so some atom must be left without an octet.\nBCl$_3$ has 24 valence electrons. It is an incomplete-octet case, not an odd-electron case.\nSF$_6$ has 48 valence electrons and shows an expanded octet.\nCO$_2$ has 16 valence electrons and obeys the octet rule.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-081","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":81,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Two hydrogen atoms A and B, with nuclei $N_A$, $N_B$ and electrons $e_A$, $e_B$, approach each other to form $H_2$. Which of the following interactions is repulsive?","image":"","options":["$N_A - e_B$","$N_B - e_A$","$e_A - e_B$","$N_A - e_A$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Attractive forces act between a nucleus and an electron. This covers each nucleus with its own electron ($N_A-e_A$, $N_B-e_B$) and each nucleus with the other atom's electron ($N_A-e_B$, $N_B-e_A$).\nRepulsive forces act between like charges. These are the two electrons ($e_A-e_B$) and the two nuclei ($N_A-N_B$).\nSo $e_A-e_B$ is the repulsive interaction.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-082","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":82,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly gives the decreasing order of repulsive interactions between electron pairs in the valence shell of a central atom?","image":"","options":["bond pair–bond pair > lone pair–bond pair > lone pair–lone pair","lone pair–bond pair > lone pair–lone pair > bond pair–bond pair","lone pair–lone pair > bond pair–bond pair > lone pair–bond pair","lone pair–lone pair > lone pair–bond pair > bond pair–bond pair"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A lone pair is held by the central atom alone, so it is localised there and occupies more space than a bond pair. A bond pair is shared between two atoms.\nRepulsion is therefore greatest when two lone pairs are involved and least between two bond pairs.\nThe order is lp–lp > lp–bp > bp–bp.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-083","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":83,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) In the Lewis structure of $O_3$, the central oxygen atom carries a formal charge of +1.\n(ii) For cations of the same size and charge, one with configuration $(n-1)d^n ns^0$ is more polarising than one with a noble gas configuration.\n(iii) The magnitude of hydrogen bonding is maximum in the gaseous state.\n(iv) In $SF_4$, the lone pair occupies an axial position to minimise repulsion.\n(v) An antibonding molecular orbital has a nodal plane between the nuclei where electron density is zero.\n(vi) The bond angle in $H_2O$ is smaller than in $NH_3$ because lone pair–lone pair repulsion exceeds lone pair–bond pair repulsion.","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the central O has formal charge 6 − 2 − (1/2)(6) = +1.\n(ii) Correct: this is a Fajans rule.\n(iii) Incorrect: hydrogen bonding is maximum in the solid state and minimum in the gaseous state.\n(iv) Incorrect: the equatorial lone pair is more stable, since it has only two lp–bp repulsions at 90°.\n(v) Correct: an antibonding MO has a nodal plane between the nuclei.\n(vi) Correct: the angles are 104.5° for $H_2O$ and 107° for $NH_3$.\nSo 4 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-084","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":84,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following can form intermolecular hydrogen bonds among their own molecules?\nHF, H$_2$O, NH$_3$, CH$_4$, C$_2$H$_5$OH, CH$_3$OCH$_3$, CCl$_4$","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Self-association needs an H bonded directly to F, O or N, plus an electronegative atom on a neighbouring molecule to receive the bond.\nHF has an F–H bond, H$_2$O has O–H bonds, NH$_3$ has N–H bonds and C$_2$H$_5$OH has an O–H bond. All four can form hydrogen bonds with their own molecules.\nCH$_4$ has only C–H bonds. CCl$_4$ has no H at all.\nCH$_3$OCH$_3$ contains O, but all its H atoms are bonded to C, so it cannot hydrogen bond with itself.\nThe total is 4.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-085","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":85,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following species are paramagnetic?\n$B_2$, $C_2$, $N_2$, $O_2$, $O_2^-$, $O_2^{2-}$, $F_2$, $O_2^+$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"$B_2$: the last two electrons occupy $\\pi 2p_x$ and $\\pi 2p_y$ singly, so it is paramagnetic.\n$C_2$ and $N_2$: all electrons paired, so diamagnetic.\n$O_2$: 2 unpaired electrons in $\\pi^*$, paramagnetic. $O_2^-$: 1 unpaired, paramagnetic. $O_2^+$: 1 unpaired, paramagnetic.\n$O_2^{2-}$ and $F_2$: $\\pi^*$ orbitals completely filled, so diamagnetic.\nParamagnetic species are $B_2$, $O_2$, $O_2^-$ and $O_2^+$, giving 4.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-086","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":86,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"A graph of potential energy against internuclear distance for two approaching H atoms falls to a minimum and then rises sharply at shorter distances. At the minimum, the internuclear distance and the energy released per mole of $H_2$ formed are respectively:","image":"","options":["74 pm and 435.8 kJ mol$^{-1}$","144 pm and 435.8 kJ mol$^{-1}$","74 pm and 155 kJ mol$^{-1}$","109 pm and 946 kJ mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The minimum of the curve is the most stable state of $H_2$. Here the net attraction balances the repulsion.\nThe distance at the minimum is the bond length of $H_2$, which is 74 pm.\nThe energy released there is the bond enthalpy, 435.8 kJ mol$^{-1}$.\n144 pm and 155 kJ mol$^{-1}$ belong to $F_2$; 109 pm and 946 kJ mol$^{-1}$ belong to $N_2$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-087","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":87,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (species) with List-II (hybridisation of the central atom).\nList-I:\n(A) $BeCl_2$\n(B) $BCl_3$\n(C) $PCl_5$\n(D) $[Co(NH_3)_6]^{3+}$\nList-II:\n(I) $sp^3d$\n(II) $d^2sp^3$\n(III) $sp$\n(IV) $sp^2$","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) $BeCl_2$: Be uses one 2s and one 2p orbital, giving sp hybrids and a linear molecule (III).\n(B) $BCl_3$: B uses one s and two p orbitals, giving $sp^2$ hybrids and a trigonal planar molecule (IV).\n(C) $PCl_5$: P uses one s, three p and one d orbital, giving $sp^3d$ hybrids and a trigonal bipyramid (I).\n(D) $[Co(NH_3)_6]^{3+}$: Co uses inner d orbitals, giving $d^2sp^3$ hybrids and an octahedron (II).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-088","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":88,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"o-Nitrophenol and p-nitrophenol have the same molecular formula. Which statement correctly compares their boiling points?","image":"","options":["o-Nitrophenol boils higher because intramolecular hydrogen bonding joins its molecules together","p-Nitrophenol boils higher because its molecules associate through intermolecular hydrogen bonds, while in o-nitrophenol the OH hydrogen is engaged within the same molecule","Both boil at the same temperature because they contain the same functional groups","o-Nitrophenol boils higher because the closeness of its –OH and –NO$_2$ groups gives stronger intermolecular hydrogen bonds"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In o-nitrophenol, the OH hydrogen lies between two oxygen atoms of the same molecule, giving an intramolecular hydrogen bond (NCERT Fig. 4.22).\nBecause that hydrogen is already used up, the o-nitrophenol molecules hardly associate with one another.\nIn p-nitrophenol the two groups are too far apart to bond within the molecule. Its OH therefore hydrogen bonds to neighbouring molecules, and more energy is needed to separate them. Its boiling point is higher.\nOption (1) is wrong because an intramolecular bond does not join separate molecules.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-089","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":89,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"The H–O–H bond angle in the water molecule is approximately:","image":"","options":["$109.5^\\circ$","$107^\\circ$","$104.5^\\circ$","$120^\\circ$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"$H_2O$ has two bond pairs and two lone pairs on O, arranged tetrahedrally.\nThe strong lp–lp repulsion, together with lp–bp repulsion, squeezes the bond pairs together. This reduces the angle from $109.5^\\circ$ to $104.5^\\circ$.\nThe value $107^\\circ$ belongs to $NH_3$, which has only one lone pair.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-090","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":90,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following processes has an enthalpy change equal to the bond enthalpy of HCl (431.0 kJ $mol^{-1}$)?","image":"","options":["$HCl(aq) \\rightarrow H^+(aq) + Cl^-(aq)$","$\\frac{1}{2}H_2(g) + \\frac{1}{2}Cl_2(g) \\rightarrow HCl(g)$","$HCl(g) \\rightarrow H^+(g) + Cl^-(g)$","$HCl(g) \\rightarrow H(g) + Cl(g)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Bond enthalpy is the energy needed to break one mole of bonds of a particular type between two atoms in the gaseous state.\nThe products must therefore be neutral gaseous atoms: $HCl(g) \\rightarrow H(g) + Cl(g)$, with $\\Delta_aH$ = 431.0 kJ $mol^{-1}$.\nOption (3) forms gaseous ions, which is heterolytic cleavage, not bond dissociation into atoms.\nOption (2) is the formation of HCl, and option (1) is ionisation in water.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-091","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":91,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"The H–H bond enthalpy is 435.8 kJ mol$^{-1}$. How much energy is released when 0.50 mol of gaseous hydrogen atoms combine completely to form $H_2(g)$?","image":"","options":["108.95 kJ","217.9 kJ","435.8 kJ","871.6 kJ"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: n(H atoms) = 0.50 mol; H–H bond enthalpy = 435.8 kJ mol$^{-1}$.\nFormula: 2H → $H_2$, so moles of $H_2$ formed = n(H)/2. Energy released = n($H_2$) × 435.8 kJ.\nSubstituting: n($H_2$) = 0.25 mol, so energy = 0.25 × 435.8 kJ.\nSo the energy released = 108.95 kJ.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-092","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":92,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The formation of solid NaCl from gaseous Na atoms and gaseous Cl atoms is energetically favourable.\nReason (R): The sum of the ionization enthalpy of Na and the electron gain enthalpy of Cl is negative.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. The lattice enthalpy released on forming solid NaCl (788 kJ $mol^{-1}$) is larger than the net energy that must be supplied to form the gaseous ions.\nR is false. The ionization enthalpy of Na is 495.8 kJ $mol^{-1}$ and the electron gain enthalpy of Cl is –348.7 kJ $mol^{-1}$. Their sum is +147.1 kJ $mol^{-1}$, which is positive, so energy is absorbed.\nThe stability of an ionic solid therefore comes from lattice formation, not from the ions merely completing an octet in the gas phase.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-093","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":93,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the homonuclear diatomic species: $O_2$, $F_2$, $N_2$, $C_2$, $B_2$, $Be_2$.\nAccording to NCERT, for how many of these is the energy sequence $\\sigma 2s < \\sigma^* 2s < \\sigma 2p_z < (\\pi 2p_x = \\pi 2p_y) < (\\pi^* 2p_x = \\pi^* 2p_y) < \\sigma^* 2p_z$ the correct one?","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In the given sequence, $\\sigma 2p_z$ lies below the $\\pi 2p$ MOs.\nNCERT states that this sequence applies to $O_2$ and $F_2$.\nIt is not correct for $Li_2$, $Be_2$, $B_2$, $C_2$ and $N_2$. For these, the $\\pi 2p_x$ and $\\pi 2p_y$ MOs lie below $\\sigma 2p_z$.\nOnly $O_2$ and $F_2$ from the list fit the given order, which gives 2.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-094","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":94,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The formation of an ionic compound is mainly favoured by the attainment of noble gas octets by the ions in the gaseous state, which is an energy-releasing step.\nStatement II: According to NCERT, the enthalpy of lattice formation gives a qualitative measure of the stability of an ionic compound.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. For NaCl, forming the gaseous ions with octets absorbs a net +147.1 kJ mol$^{-1}$, so it is not an energy-releasing step. NCERT states that stability is not explained simply by achieving octets in the gaseous state.\nStatement II is correct. NCERT says a qualitative measure of the stability of an ionic compound is provided by its enthalpy of lattice formation.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-095","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":95,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"The dipole moment of water is 1.85 D. Taking 1 D = $3.33564 \\times 10^{-30}$ C m, what is its value in SI units?","image":"","options":["$6.17 \\times 10^{-30}$ C m","$1.80 \\times 10^{-30}$ C m","$0.555 \\times 10^{-30}$ C m","$61.7 \\times 10^{-30}$ C m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\mu$ = 1.85 D and 1 D = $3.33564 \\times 10^{-30}$ C m.\nFormula: $\\mu$(C m) = $\\mu$(D) × $3.33564 \\times 10^{-30}$.\nSubstituting: 1.85 × 3.33564 = 6.17.\nSo $\\mu$ = $6.17 \\times 10^{-30}$ C m.\nThe value 0.555 comes from dividing instead of multiplying.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-096","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":96,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: CuCl shows more covalent character than NaCl.\nStatement II: For cations of similar size and charge, a cation with the $(n-1)d^{n}ns^{0}$ configuration is more polarising than one with the noble gas configuration $ns^{2}np^{6}$.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement II is correct. It is Fajans' rule on electronic configuration: d electrons shield the nuclear charge poorly, so such cations polarise anions more strongly.\nStatement I is correct. $Cu^+$ is $3d^{10}4s^0$, while $Na^+$ is $2s^22p^6$. $Cu^+$ is therefore more polarising, and CuCl is more covalent than NaCl.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-097","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":97,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (species) with List-II (behaviour with respect to the octet rule).\nList-I:\n(a) $BCl_3$\n(b) $NO_2$\n(c) $SF_6$\n(d) $SCl_2$\nList-II:\n(i) Expanded octet on central atom\n(ii) Central atom obeys the octet rule\n(iii) Incomplete octet on central atom\n(iv) Odd-electron molecule","image":"","options":["a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii","a-iv, b-iii, c-i, d-ii","a-iii, b-iv, c-ii, d-i","a-i, b-iv, c-iii, d-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) $BCl_3$: B has only 6 electrons around it, an incomplete octet, so (iii).\n(b) $NO_2$: 5 + 12 = 17 electrons, which is odd, so (iv).\n(c) $SF_6$: S has 12 electrons around it, an expanded octet, so (i).\n(d) $SCl_2$: S has exactly 8 electrons, so the octet is obeyed, (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-098","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":98,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In $ClF_3$, chlorine has three bond pairs and two lone pairs. The two lone pairs occupy equatorial positions of a trigonal bipyramid, so the molecule is T-shaped.\nStatement II: $BrF_5$ is square planar because its central atom has five bond pairs and one lone pair.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Cl has 5 electron pairs, so they are arranged as a trigonal bipyramid. Placing both lone pairs equatorially gives the fewest lp–bp repulsions at 90°. The three F atoms then form a T-shape.\nStatement II is incorrect. With 6 electron pairs (5 bp + 1 lp) arranged octahedrally, removing one vertex for the lone pair leaves a square pyramidal shape, not a square planar one.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-099","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":99,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following species have a bond order of 3?\n$N_2$, $CO$, $NO^+$, $CN^-$, $O_2^{2-}$, $F_2$, $O_2^+$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Isoelectronic species have identical bond orders.\n$N_2$, $CO$, $NO^+$ and $CN^-$ each have 14 electrons, so each has bond order 3. That makes 4 species.\n$O_2^{2-}$ and $F_2$ each have 18 electrons, so each has bond order 1.\n$O_2^+$ (15 electrons) has bond order $\\frac{10-5}{2}=2.5$.\nSo 4 species have bond order 3.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-100","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":100,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"When the 2s and 2p atomic orbitals of two identical second-period atoms combine by LCAO, how many molecular orbitals are formed, and how many of them are bonding?","image":"","options":["4 MOs, all of them bonding","8 MOs, of which 4 are bonding","8 MOs, of which 5 are bonding","16 MOs, of which 8 are bonding"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The number of MOs formed always equals the number of atomic orbitals that combine.\nEach atom has one 2s and three 2p orbitals, so two atoms supply 8 AOs and form 8 MOs.\nEvery pair of combining AOs gives one bonding and one antibonding MO.\nThe bonding MOs are $\\sigma 2s$, $\\sigma 2p_z$, $\\pi 2p_x$ and $\\pi 2p_y$. The other four are their antibonding partners.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-101","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":101,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"According to molecular orbital theory, which of the following species has a bond order of zero and therefore does not exist as a stable molecule?","image":"","options":["$H_2$","$Li_2$","$He_2$","$C_2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Bond order = $\\frac{1}{2}(N_b - N_a)$.\n$He_2$ has 4 electrons: $(\\sigma 1s)^2(\\sigma^* 1s)^2$, so $N_b = 2$ and $N_a = 2$. Its bond order is 0, so it is unstable.\n$H_2$ has bond order 1, $Li_2$ has 1 and $C_2$ has 2. All three are stable.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-102","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":102,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"In applying VSEPR theory to the $CO_2$ molecule, each C=O double bond is:","image":"","options":["treated as two separate electron pairs, giving four electron pairs around C","treated as a single super pair, giving a linear shape","ignored, since only lone pairs decide the shape","treated as a lone pair on carbon"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"VSEPR treats a multiple bond as if it were a single electron pair (a 'super pair').\nCarbon in $CO_2$ therefore has two such super pairs and no lone pair.\nTwo electron domains are as far apart as possible at $180^\\circ$, so $CO_2$ is linear.\nCounting each double bond as two pairs would wrongly predict a tetrahedral arrangement.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-103","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":103,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about molecular orbital theory is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["An electron in a molecular orbital is influenced by two or more nuclei, so a molecular orbital is polycentric.","The total energy of the two molecular orbitals formed equals the total energy of the two atomic orbitals that combined.","Molecular orbitals are filled according to the aufbau principle, Pauli exclusion principle and Hund's rule.","The antibonding molecular orbital has lower energy, and hence greater stability, than the corresponding bonding molecular orbital."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: an atomic orbital is monocentric, while a molecular orbital is polycentric.\n(2) Correct: the bonding MO is lowered and the antibonding MO is raised, but the total energy of the pair stays the same as that of the two AOs.\n(3) Correct: these are the filling rules for MOs.\n(4) Incorrect: it is the bonding MO that has lower energy and greater stability.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-104","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":104,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"For gaseous water, the enthalpies needed to break the first and second O–H bonds are 502 kJ $mol^{-1}$ and 427 kJ $mol^{-1}$ respectively:\n$H_2O(g) \\rightarrow H(g) + OH(g)$; $\\Delta_aH_1$ = 502 kJ $mol^{-1}$\n$OH(g) \\rightarrow H(g) + O(g)$; $\\Delta_aH_2$ = 427 kJ $mol^{-1}$\nWhat is the mean (average) O–H bond enthalpy in water?","image":"","options":["502 kJ $mol^{-1}$","427 kJ $mol^{-1}$","464.5 kJ $mol^{-1}$","929 kJ $mol^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $\\Delta_aH_1$ = 502 kJ $mol^{-1}$ and $\\Delta_aH_2$ = 427 kJ $mol^{-1}$. Two O–H bonds are broken.\nFormula: mean bond enthalpy = total bond dissociation enthalpy ÷ number of bonds broken.\nSubstituting: (502 + 427)/2 = 929/2.\nSo the mean O–H bond enthalpy = 464.5 kJ $mol^{-1}$. The value 929 is the total for both bonds, not the average.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-105","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":105,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following molecules have zero dipole moment?\n$CO_2$, $BF_3$, $CH_4$, $CCl_4$, $CHCl_3$, $H_2S$, $NF_3$, $H_2$","image":"","options":["4","5","6","3"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Zero dipole moment:\n$CO_2$ (linear), $BF_3$ (trigonal planar), $CH_4$ and $CCl_4$ (symmetric tetrahedral), and $H_2$ (nonpolar bond). That makes 5.\nNon-zero dipole moment:\n$CHCl_3$ (1.04 D): its tetrahedron is unsymmetrical.\n$H_2S$ (0.95 D): bent.\n$NF_3$ (0.23 D): pyramidal.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-106","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":106,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"In the valence bond treatment of $H_2$ formation, two hydrogen atoms A and B, with electrons $e_A$ and $e_B$, approach each other. Which of the following interactions is a repulsive force?","image":"","options":["Nucleus $N_A$ and electron $e_A$","Nucleus $N_A$ and electron $e_B$","Electron $e_A$ and electron $e_B$","Nucleus $N_B$ and electron $e_A$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Attractive forces act between each nucleus and its own electron ($N_A$–$e_A$, $N_B$–$e_B$). They also act between each nucleus and the other atom's electron ($N_A$–$e_B$, $N_B$–$e_A$).\nRepulsive forces act between the two electrons ($e_A$–$e_B$) and between the two nuclei ($N_A$–$N_B$).\nOptions (1), (2) and (4) are all nucleus–electron attractions. Only (3) is a repulsion.\nThe new attractions outweigh the new repulsions, so the atoms come together until the energy is minimum at 74 pm.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-107","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":107,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements:\n(i) The double bond in the $C_2$ molecule consists of two $\\pi$ bonds.\n(ii) In $PCl_5$, the axial P–Cl bonds are shorter and stronger than the equatorial bonds.\n(iii) The H–O–H bond angle in water is smaller than the H–N–H angle in ammonia because lone pair–lone pair repulsion exceeds lone pair–bond pair repulsion.\n(iv) In $N_2$ the $\\sigma2p_z$ orbital lies higher in energy than the $\\pi2p_x$ and $\\pi2p_y$ orbitals, but in $O_2$ it lies lower.\n(v) The energy of a resonance hybrid is higher than that of any of its canonical structures.\nWhich of the statements are correct?","image":"","options":["(i), (iii) and (iv) only","(ii), (iii) and (v) only","(i), (ii) and (iv) only","(iii), (iv) and (v) only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the 4 electrons of $C_2$ occupy the two $\\pi$ MOs, so its double bond is made of two $\\pi$ bonds.\n(ii) Incorrect: axial bonds suffer more repulsion from the equatorial bond pairs, so they are longer and weaker.\n(iii) Correct: water has two lone pairs and a bond angle of $104.5^\\circ$; ammonia has one lone pair and a bond angle of $107^\\circ$.\n(iv) Correct: the MO energy order changes after $N_2$.\n(v) Incorrect: resonance lowers energy, so the hybrid is more stable than any canonical form.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-108","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":108,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following species has the same bond order as the $N_2$ molecule?","image":"","options":["$O_2$","$NO^+$","$F_2$","$O_2^{2-}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Isoelectronic species have identical bond orders.\n$N_2$, $CO$ and $NO^+$ each have 14 electrons, so each has bond order 3.\n$O_2$ has bond order 2.\n$F_2$ and $O_2^{2-}$ are isoelectronic with each other (18 electrons) and have bond order 1.\nSo only $NO^+$ matches $N_2$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-109","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":109,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following molecules has zero dipole moment even though its individual bonds are polar?","image":"","options":["$NH_3$","$H_2O$","$BF_3$","$NF_3$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"$BF_3$ is trigonal planar, with the B–F bonds at $120^\\circ$ to each other. The resultant of any two bond moments is equal and opposite to the third, so the net dipole moment is zero.\n$NH_3$ and $NF_3$ are pyramidal and $H_2O$ is bent. In these shapes the bond dipoles do not cancel, so each of them has a non-zero dipole moment.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-110","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":110,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"The valence bond theory, which explains covalent bond formation using quantum mechanical principles, was introduced by:","image":"","options":["Heitler and London","Hund and Mulliken","Sidgwick and Powell","Kössel and Lewis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Valence bond theory was introduced by Heitler and London and developed further by Pauling and others.\nHund and Mulliken developed molecular orbital theory.\nSidgwick and Powell proposed the VSEPR idea.\nKössel and Lewis gave the electronic (octet) approach to bonding.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-111","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":111,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"In solid chlorine, the Cl–Cl bond length within a molecule is 198 pm. The distance between the nuclei of the nearest Cl atoms belonging to two adjacent $Cl_2$ molecules is 360 pm. By how much does the van der Waals radius of chlorine exceed its covalent radius?","image":"","options":["162 pm","81 pm","261 pm","40.5 pm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: bond length = 198 pm; non-bonded internuclear distance between adjacent molecules = 360 pm.\nFormula: covalent radius $r_c$ = bond length/2. Van der Waals radius $r_{vdw}$ = (distance between similar atoms in separate molecules)/2.\nSubstituting: $r_c$ = 198/2 = 99 pm and $r_{vdw}$ = 360/2 = 180 pm.\nSo $r_{vdw} - r_c$ = 180 − 99 = 81 pm. The value 162 pm wrongly forgets to halve the distances.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-112","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":112,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Consider one Lewis structure of ozone, O=O–O, in which every atom has an octet. What is the formal charge on the central oxygen atom?","image":"","options":["0","+1","−1","+2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Formula: formal charge = valence electrons − lone-pair electrons − ½ × bonding electrons.\nThe central O has 6 valence electrons, one lone pair (2 electrons) and three bonds (6 bonding electrons).\nSubstituting: 6 − 2 − ½ × 6 = +1.\nAs a check, the double-bonded end O comes to 0 and the single-bonded end O to −1, so the charges add up to zero for neutral O$_3$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-113","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":113,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The bond length order is $O_2^{2-} > O_2^- > O_2 > O_2^+$.\nStatement II: The peroxide ion $O_2^{2-}$ is paramagnetic.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The bond orders are $O_2^{2-}$ = 1, $O_2^-$ = 1.5, $O_2$ = 2 and $O_2^+$ = 2.5.\nBond length decreases as bond order increases. So $O_2^{2-}$ has the longest bond and $O_2^+$ the shortest, and Statement I is correct.\nIn $O_2^{2-}$, the $\\pi^* 2p_x$ and $\\pi^* 2p_y$ orbitals are both doubly occupied. With no unpaired electrons it is diamagnetic, so Statement II is incorrect.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-114","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":114,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"The Lewis symbol of a second-period element X shows six dots around the symbol. What is the common (group) valence of X?","image":"","options":["6","2","8","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The number of dots in a Lewis symbol equals the number of valence electrons. X has 6.\nNCERT: the group valence is either the number of dots or 8 minus the number of dots.\nAn atom with 6 valence electrons needs only 2 more to complete an octet, so its valence is 8 − 6 = 2. X is oxygen, which is divalent.\nOption 6 counts the electrons, not the valence.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-115","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":115,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A sigma bond is stronger than a pi bond formed between the same two atoms.\nReason (R): In axial (head-on) overlap, the orbitals overlap to a larger extent than in sidewise overlap.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Bond strength depends on the extent of overlap: greater overlap gives a stronger bond.\nIn a $\\sigma$ bond, orbitals overlap head-on along the internuclear axis, so the overlap is large. In a $\\pi$ bond, the sidewise overlap is smaller.\nSo A is true, R is true, and R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-116","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":116,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The dipole moment of $NH_3$ (1.47 D) is greater than that of $NF_3$ (0.23 D).\nReason (R): Fluorine is more electronegative than nitrogen.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true: the values come from NCERT Table 4.5.\nR is true: F is more electronegative than N.\nR does not explain A. Taken alone, it would suggest $NF_3$ should be more polar.\nThe real reason is the lone pair. In $NH_3$, the lone-pair orbital dipole adds to the resultant of the N–H bond moments. In $NF_3$, it opposes the resultant of the N–F bond moments and largely cancels it.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-117","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":117,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"For the water molecule, the enthalpies needed to break the first and second O–H bonds are:\n$H_2O(g) \\rightarrow H(g) + OH(g)$; $\\Delta_a H_1 = 502$ kJ mol$^{-1}$\n$OH(g) \\rightarrow H(g) + O(g)$; $\\Delta_a H_2 = 427$ kJ mol$^{-1}$\nWhat is the mean (average) O–H bond enthalpy in water?","image":"","options":["464.5 kJ mol$^{-1}$","929.0 kJ mol$^{-1}$","75.0 kJ mol$^{-1}$","427.0 kJ mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\Delta_a H_1 = 502$ kJ mol$^{-1}$ and $\\Delta_a H_2 = 427$ kJ mol$^{-1}$ for breaking the two O–H bonds in turn.\nFormula: mean bond enthalpy = total bond dissociation enthalpy ÷ number of bonds broken.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{502 + 427}{2} = \\frac{929}{2}$.\nSo the mean O–H bond enthalpy = 464.5 kJ mol$^{-1}$.\nThe value 929 kJ mol$^{-1}$ is the total for both bonds, not the average per bond.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-118","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":118,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"According to molecular orbital theory, what are the bond order and the magnetic nature of the $Li_2$ molecule?","image":"","options":["1, diamagnetic","1, paramagnetic","0.5, paramagnetic","2, diamagnetic"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Li has 3 electrons, so $Li_2$ has 6 electrons.\nConfiguration: $\\sigma1s^2\\,\\sigma^*1s^2\\,\\sigma2s^2$, so $N_b = 4$ and $N_a = 2$.\nBond order $= \\frac{1}{2}(N_b - N_a) = \\frac{1}{2}(4-2) = 1$.\nEvery occupied MO holds a pair of electrons, so no electron is unpaired and $Li_2$ is diamagnetic. NCERT notes that diamagnetic $Li_2$ molecules exist in the vapour phase.\nOption (2) fails because $Li_2$ has no singly occupied orbital.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-119","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":119,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about hydrogen bonding are correct?\n(i) In an X–H bond, where X is highly electronegative, the shared electron pair shifts towards X.\n(ii) A hydrogen bond is stronger than a covalent bond.\n(iii) Hydrogen bonds can exist between molecules of different compounds.\n(iv) An intramolecular hydrogen bond requires at least two separate molecules.\n(v) The hydrogen atom involved acquires a fractional positive charge.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the electrons move towards the more electronegative atom X.\n(ii) Incorrect: NCERT states that a hydrogen bond is weaker than a covalent bond.\n(iii) Correct: intermolecular hydrogen bonds can form between molecules of different compounds, for example ethanol and water.\n(iv) Incorrect: an intramolecular hydrogen bond forms within a single molecule.\n(v) Correct: hydrogen becomes $\\delta^+$.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-120","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":120,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"In one valid Lewis structure of the nitrite ion, $NO_2^-$ (one N=O and one N–O bond, every atom with an octet), how many lone pairs are present in total?","image":"","options":["4","5","6","7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Total valence electrons = 5 + 2(6) + 1 = 18, which is 9 pairs.\nThe bonds use 3 pairs (one double bond + one single bond), leaving 6 pairs as lone pairs.\nCheck by atom: the doubly bonded O has 2 lone pairs, the singly bonded O has 3, and N has 1. That is 6 in total, and each atom has an octet.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-121","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":121,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"In the ethyne ($C_2H_2$) molecule, how many $\\sigma$ bonds and $\\pi$ bonds are present in total?","image":"","options":["3 $\\sigma$ and 2 $\\pi$","2 $\\sigma$ and 3 $\\pi$","5 $\\sigma$ and 1 $\\pi$","4 $\\sigma$ and 1 $\\pi$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Each carbon in ethyne is sp hybridised.\nOne sp–sp axial overlap gives the C–C $\\sigma$ bond.\nEach carbon's second sp hybrid overlaps with H 1s, giving two C–H $\\sigma$ bonds. That makes 3 $\\sigma$ bonds in total.\nThe unhybridised $2p_y$ and $2p_z$ orbitals on the two carbons overlap sidewise to form 2 $\\pi$ bonds.\nThe option '5 $\\sigma$, 1 $\\pi$' describes ethene, not ethyne.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-122","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":122,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about ethane, ethene and ethyne are correct?\n(i) The C=C bond length in ethene (134 pm) is shorter than the C–C bond length in ethane (154 pm).\n(ii) In ethene, the H–C–H bond angle is about $117.6^\\circ$ and the H–C–C bond angle is about $121^\\circ$.\n(iii) The $\\pi$ bond in ethene is formed by sidewise overlap of unhybridised 2p orbitals, which lie perpendicular to the plane of the molecule.\n(iv) The C–H bond in ethene (108 pm) is slightly shorter than the C–H bond in ethane (109 pm).\n(v) The carbon hybrid orbital forming a C–H bond in ethene has 25% s-character.\n(vi) In ethyne, the unhybridised $2p_y$ and $2p_z$ orbitals of carbon are used to form the C–H bonds.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the double bond (sigma + pi) holds the carbons closer, giving 134 pm versus 154 pm.\n(ii) Correct: these are the NCERT values for ethene.\n(iii) Correct: the pi bond comes from p orbitals perpendicular to the molecular plane. Its electron clouds lie above and below that plane.\n(iv) Correct: an $sp^2$–s bond (108 pm) is shorter than an $sp^3$–s bond (109 pm), since more s-character means a shorter bond.\n(v) Incorrect: the carbon hybrid in ethene is $sp^2$, so its s-character is 33.3%.\n(vi) Incorrect: in ethyne the C–H bonds use sp hybrids, while $2p_y$ and $2p_z$ form the two pi bonds.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-123","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":123,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (molecule) with List-II (bond length):\nList-I\n(a) $H_2$\n(b) HF\n(c) $N_2$\n(d) $Cl_2$\nList-II\n(i) 199 pm\n(ii) 74 pm\n(iii) 109 pm\n(iv) 92 pm","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a) $H_2$: the two smallest atoms give the shortest bond, 74 pm (ii).\n(b) HF: H with small F gives 92 pm (iv).\n(c) $N_2$: the triple bond keeps the bond short despite larger atoms, 109 pm (iii).\n(d) $Cl_2$: large atoms joined by a single bond give 199 pm (i).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-124","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":124,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following pairs consists of species that have the same bond order AND are both diamagnetic?","image":"","options":["$O_2$ and $C_2$","$B_2$ and $Li_2$","$O_2^+$ and $N_2^+$","$F_2$ and $O_2^{2-}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) $O_2$ and $C_2$ both have bond order 2, but $O_2$ is paramagnetic.\n(2) $B_2$ and $Li_2$ both have bond order 1, but $B_2$ has 2 unpaired $\\pi$ electrons.\n(3) $O_2^+$ and $N_2^+$ both have bond order 2.5, but each has one unpaired electron.\n(4) $F_2$ and $O_2^{2-}$ are isoelectronic (18 electrons). Both have bond order 1, and their $\\pi^*$ orbitals are fully paired, so both are diamagnetic.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-125","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":125,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Use the following data (kJ mol$^{-1}$):\nIonization enthalpy of Na(g) = 495.8\nElectron gain enthalpy of Cl(g) = $-348.7$\nLattice enthalpy of NaCl = 788\nCalculate the enthalpy change for the process $Na(g) + Cl(g) \\rightarrow NaCl(s)$.","image":"","options":["$-640.9$ kJ mol$^{-1}$","$-935.1$ kJ mol$^{-1}$","$-292.2$ kJ mol$^{-1}$","+640.9 kJ mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\Delta_i H$ = +495.8 and $\\Delta_{eg} H$ = −348.7 kJ mol$^{-1}$. The lattice enthalpy is +788, so lattice formation releases energy: −788 kJ mol$^{-1}$.\nFormula: $\\Delta H = \\Delta_i H + \\Delta_{eg} H - \\Delta_{lattice} H$\nSubstituting: $\\Delta H$ = 495.8 − 348.7 − 788\nSo $\\Delta H$ = −640.9 kJ mol$^{-1}$. The overall process is exothermic.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-126","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":126,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Taking the z-axis as the internuclear axis, consider these pairs of orbitals on two atoms:\n(a) $1s$ and $2p_z$\n(b) $1s$ and $2p_x$\n(c) $2p_x$ and $2p_x$\n(d) $2p_z$ and $2p_x$\n(e) $2p_y$ and $2p_z$\n(f) $2s$ and $2p_z$\nHow many of these pairs give zero overlap?","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) and (f): the s orbital overlaps head-on with the $p_z$ lobe along z, giving positive overlap and a $\\sigma$ bond.\n(c): parallel $p_x$ orbitals overlap sidewise, giving positive overlap and a $\\pi$ bond.\n(b), (d), (e): positive and negative regions overlap equally, so the net overlap is zero and no bond forms.\nZero-overlap pairs = 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-127","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":127,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The dipole moment of $NH_3$ is greater than that of $NF_3$, although fluorine is more electronegative than nitrogen.\nStatement II: In $NF_3$, the orbital dipole due to the lone pair on nitrogen acts in the same direction as the resultant of the three N–F bond moments.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: $NH_3$ has the larger dipole moment.\nIn $NH_3$, the lone-pair orbital dipole points in the same direction as the resultant of the N–H bond moments, so the two add up.\nStatement II is incorrect: in $NF_3$ the lone-pair dipole opposes the resultant of the N–F bond moments. This partial cancellation lowers the net moment.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-128","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":128,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"In the methane molecule, each hybrid orbital of carbon used for a C–H bond has what percentage of s-character, and what is the angle between any two of these hybrid orbitals?","image":"","options":["33.3% s-character; $120^\\circ$","25% s-character; $109.5^\\circ$","25% s-character; $107^\\circ$","50% s-character; $180^\\circ$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Carbon in $CH_4$ mixes one 2s and three 2p orbitals, giving four $sp^3$ hybrids.\nEach hybrid contains one-fourth s, so it has 25% s-character and 75% p-character.\nThe four hybrids point to the corners of a tetrahedron, so they are $109.5^\\circ$ apart.\n$107^\\circ$ is the bond angle in $NH_3$, where a lone pair squeezes the bonds. The $33.3\\%/120^\\circ$ and $50\\%/180^\\circ$ pairs belong to $sp^2$ and $sp$ hybrids.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-129","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":129,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Water has a net dipole moment of 1.85 D and an H–O–H bond angle of $104.5^\\circ$. Treating the net dipole moment as the vector sum of two equal O–H bond moments, what is the dipole moment of a single O–H bond? (Take $\\cos 52.25^\\circ = 0.612$)","image":"","options":["1.51 D","0.93 D","3.02 D","1.17 D"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\mu_{net}$ = 1.85 D and bond angle $\\theta = 104.5^\\circ$.\nFormula: two equal bond moments $m$ give a resultant along the bisector, so $\\mu_{net} = 2m\\cos(\\theta/2)$.\nSubstituting: $m = \\frac{1.85}{2 \\times 0.612} = 1.51$ D.\nThe option 0.93 D wrongly ignores the angle (1.85/2), and 3.02 D is the sum of two bond moments, not one.\nSo the O–H bond moment is about 1.51 D.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-130","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":130,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"In the formation of CaF$_2$ by electron transfer, which option correctly gives the electrovalence of calcium and of fluorine?","image":"","options":["Calcium +2, fluorine −1","Calcium +1, fluorine −2","Calcium +2, fluorine −2","Calcium +1, fluorine −1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Calcium loses two electrons to form Ca$^{2+}$, reaching the argon configuration.\nEach fluorine gains one electron to form F$^-$, reaching the neon configuration.\nElectrovalence equals the number of unit charges on the ion, so calcium is +2 and fluorine is −1.\nThis is why two F$^-$ ions balance one Ca$^{2+}$ ion in CaF$_2$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-131","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":131,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"According to VSEPR theory, what is the geometry of the $BF_3$ molecule?","image":"","options":["Trigonal pyramidal","Trigonal planar","T-shaped","Tetrahedral"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Boron in $BF_3$ has three bond pairs and no lone pair around it.\nThree electron pairs keep as far apart as possible by pointing to the corners of an equilateral triangle, with F–B–F angles of 120°.\nTrigonal pyramidal (as in $NH_3$) would need an extra lone pair, so it does not fit $BF_3$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-132","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":132,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): $Be_2$ molecule does not exist.\nReason (R): In $Be_2$, the number of electrons in bonding molecular orbitals equals the number in antibonding molecular orbitals.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"$Be_2$ has 8 electrons: $(\\sigma 1s)^2(\\sigma^* 1s)^2(\\sigma 2s)^2(\\sigma^* 2s)^2$.\nThis gives $N_b = 4$ and $N_a = 4$, so R is true.\nBond order = $\\frac{1}{2}(4-4) = 0$. A zero bond order means the molecule is unstable, so A is true.\nR is exactly why A holds, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-133","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":133,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"In the LCAO treatment of the $H_2$ molecule, the bonding molecular orbital $\\sigma$ arises from:","image":"","options":["subtraction of the wave functions $\\psi_A$ and $\\psi_B$, with constructive interference of the electron waves","addition of $\\psi_A$ and $\\psi_B$, with destructive interference of the electron waves","addition of $\\psi_A$ and $\\psi_B$, with constructive interference of the electron waves","multiplication of $\\psi_A$ and $\\psi_B$, giving a node between the nuclei"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"LCAO gives $\\sigma = \\psi_A + \\psi_B$ for the bonding MO and $\\sigma^* = \\psi_A - \\psi_B$ for the antibonding MO.\nIn the bonding MO the electron waves reinforce each other. This is constructive interference, and it builds up electron density between the nuclei.\nDestructive interference occurs in the antibonding MO, which is formed by subtraction. That rules out options 1 and 2.\nWave functions are not multiplied in LCAO, so option 4 is also wrong.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-134","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":134,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Taking the z-axis as the internuclear axis, how many of the following pairs of atomic orbitals will form a sigma ($\\sigma$) bond?\n(i) 1s and 1s\n(ii) 1s and $2p_z$\n(iii) $2p_z$ and $2p_z$\n(iv) $2p_y$ and $2p_y$\n(v) 1s and $2p_x$\n(vi) $2p_x$ and $2p_y$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) 1s and 1s: head-on s–s overlap gives a $\\sigma$ bond.\n(ii) 1s and $2p_z$: s–p overlap along the axis gives a $\\sigma$ bond.\n(iii) $2p_z$ and $2p_z$: head-on p–p overlap gives a $\\sigma$ bond.\n(iv) $2p_y$ and $2p_y$: their axes are parallel to each other and perpendicular to z, so the overlap is sidewise. This gives a $\\pi$ bond, not a $\\sigma$ bond.\n(v) 1s and $2p_x$, and (vi) $2p_x$ and $2p_y$: the positive and negative lobes cancel, giving zero overlap and no bond.\nSo three pairs form $\\sigma$ bonds.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-135","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":135,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"How many canonical forms are used in NCERT to describe the carbonate ion, $CO_3^{2-}$, as a resonance hybrid?","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A single Lewis structure of $CO_3^{2-}$ has one C=O and two C–O bonds, which makes the bonds unequal.\nExperiments show that all three C–O bonds are equivalent.\nThe double bond can be placed on any one of the three oxygens, giving three canonical forms (I, II, III).\nThe hybrid of these three forms describes the ion.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-136","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":136,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is an odd-electron molecule, in which the octet rule cannot be satisfied for all atoms?","image":"","options":["CO","$N_2$","$CO_2$","NO"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Count the total valence electrons in each molecule.\nNO: 5 + 6 = 11, which is odd. So at least one atom cannot complete an octet. NCERT cites NO and $NO_2$ as odd-electron molecules.\nCO: 4 + 6 = 10. $N_2$: 10. $CO_2$: 16. All three are even, and all their atoms satisfy the octet.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-137","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":137,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In o-nitrophenol, the hydrogen of the –OH group forms an intermolecular hydrogen bond with the nitro group of a neighbouring molecule.\nStatement II: The strength of hydrogen bonding in a compound is maximum in the solid state and minimum in the gaseous state.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. In o-nitrophenol, the H atom lies between two oxygen atoms of the same molecule. This makes it the standard example of an intramolecular hydrogen bond, not an intermolecular one.\nStatement II is correct. NCERT states that the magnitude of hydrogen bonding is maximum in the solid state and minimum in the gaseous state.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-138","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":138,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the reaction $AlCl_3 + Cl^- \\rightarrow AlCl_4^-$.\nStatement I: The hybridisation of Al changes from $sp^2$ to $sp^3$ in this reaction.\nStatement II: The shape around Al changes from trigonal planar to square planar because a fourth orbital is now used.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In $AlCl_3$, Al (like B in $BCl_3$) uses one 3s and two 3p orbitals. This is $sp^2$ hybridisation, giving a trigonal planar shape.\nThe incoming $Cl^-$ donates an electron pair into the empty 3p orbital of Al.\nNow four orbitals (s + three p) are involved, so Al becomes $sp^3$ hybridised. Statement I is correct.\nFour $sp^3$ hybrids point to the corners of a tetrahedron, not a square. Square planar geometry needs $dsp^2$ hybridisation. Statement II is incorrect.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-139","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":139,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The $SO_2$ molecule is bent, with a bond angle of about $119.5^\\circ$, slightly less than $120^\\circ$.\nStatement II: The central sulphur atom in $SO_2$ carries two lone pairs, as in $H_2O$.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. $SO_2$ is of the type AB2E: the double bonds count as super pairs, and S has one lone pair.\nThis gives a trigonal planar arrangement of electron domains. The stronger lp–bp repulsion bends the molecule and reduces the angle from $120^\\circ$ to $119.5^\\circ$.\nStatement II is incorrect. S in $SO_2$ has only one lone pair. If it had two lone pairs, the angle would be near $104.5^\\circ$, as in water.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-140","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":140,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The dipole moment of $NF_3$ is much smaller than that of $NH_3$, although both are pyramidal.\nReason (R): Fluorine is less electronegative than nitrogen, so the N–F bond dipoles point towards nitrogen.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true: the dipole moment of $NH_3$ (1.47 D) is greater than that of $NF_3$ (0.23 D).\nR is false: fluorine is more electronegative than nitrogen.\nThe real reason is the lone pair. In $NF_3$, the lone-pair orbital dipole opposes the resultant of the N–F bond moments. In $NH_3$, it adds to the resultant of the bond moments.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-141","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":141,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["In the $C_2$ molecule, the double bond consists of two $\\pi$ bonds and no $\\sigma$ bond","Promotion of an electron is an essential condition before hybridisation can occur","For $N_2$, the $\\sigma 2p_z$ molecular orbital is higher in energy than the $\\pi 2p_x$ and $\\pi 2p_y$ orbitals","In ethene, the H–C–H bond angle is 117.6° while the H–C–C angle is 121°"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the four electrons of $C_2$ beyond KK$\\sigma 2s^2 \\sigma^* 2s^2$ fill the two $\\pi$ MOs, so the double bond is made of two $\\pi$ bonds.\n(2) Incorrect: NCERT states that promotion of an electron is not an essential condition for hybridisation. Even filled orbitals may hybridise.\n(3) Correct: for $B_2$, $C_2$ and $N_2$, $\\sigma 2p_z$ lies above the $\\pi 2p$ orbitals.\n(4) Correct: these are the NCERT values for ethene.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-142","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":142,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (molecule) with List-II (dipole moment, as given in NCERT Table 4.5).\nList-I:\n(A) $H_2O$\n(B) $NH_3$\n(C) $CHCl_3$\n(D) $H_2S$\nList-II:\n(I) 0.95 D\n(II) 1.04 D\n(III) 1.47 D\n(IV) 1.85 D","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) $H_2O$, bent: 1.85 D, so A-IV.\n(B) $NH_3$, trigonal pyramidal: 1.47 D, so B-III.\n(C) $CHCl_3$, tetrahedral but unsymmetrical: 1.04 D, so C-II.\n(D) $H_2S$, bent: 0.95 D, so D-I. Its value is lower than that of $H_2O$ because S is much less electronegative than O.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-143","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":143,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the postulates of VSEPR theory is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The shape of a molecule depends on the number of valence shell electron pairs, bonded or nonbonded, around the central atom.","Electron pairs take up positions in space that minimise repulsion and maximise the distance between them.","A double bond is counted as two separate electron pairs when predicting the shape of a molecule.","The valence shell is taken as a sphere, with the electron pairs localised on its surface at maximum distance from one another."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: this is the basic postulate of VSEPR theory.\n(2) Correct: electron pairs repel one another and move as far apart as possible.\n(3) Incorrect: the two or three electron pairs of a multiple bond are treated together as a single super pair, not as separate pairs.\n(4) Correct: VSEPR theory models the valence shell as a sphere.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-144","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":144,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In $SF_4$, the lone pair on sulphur occupies an equatorial position of the trigonal bipyramid.\nReason (R): A lone pair in an equatorial position has only two lone pair–bond pair repulsions at $90^\\circ$, whereas in an axial position it would have three.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. $SF_4$ (AB4E) places its lone pair equatorially, which gives the see-saw shape.\nR is true. An axial lone pair would have three bond pairs at $90^\\circ$, while an equatorial lone pair has only two.\nFewer strong lp–bp repulsions at $90^\\circ$ make the equatorial arrangement more stable. R therefore correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-145","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":145,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"In chemistry, the dipole moment of a polar bond such as H–F is shown on the Lewis structure with a crossed arrow. Which description of this arrow is correct?","image":"","options":["The cross is on the positive end and the arrowhead points to the negative end","The cross is on the negative end and the arrowhead points to the positive end","The arrowhead always points towards the less electronegative atom","The arrow shows the direction of the conventional dipole moment vector"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In the chemist's crossed arrow, the cross sits on the positive end (H in HF) and the head points to the negative end (F).\nThe arrow therefore shows the direction in which electron density shifts.\nThis is opposite to the conventional dipole moment vector, which runs from the negative centre to the positive centre. That is why options 2 and 4 fail.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-146","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":146,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"In the Lewis structure of ozone, the central O atom forms one double bond and one single bond and carries one lone pair. The formal charge on the central oxygen atom is:","image":"","options":["0","−1","+2","+1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Formula: formal charge = valence electrons − non-bonding electrons − ½(bonding electrons).\nThe central O has 6 valence electrons, 2 lone-pair electrons and 6 bonding electrons (one double bond + one single bond).\nSubstituting: 6 − 2 − ½(6) = +1.\nThe singly bonded terminal O is 6 − 6 − 1 = −1, and the doubly bonded one is 0. So the net charge of the molecule is 0.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-147","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":147,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"For water, the enthalpy needed to break the first O–H bond (H$_2$O → H + OH) is 502 kJ mol$^{-1}$, and that for the second (OH → O + H) is 427 kJ mol$^{-1}$. What is the mean O–H bond enthalpy in water?","image":"","options":["929 kJ mol$^{-1}$","502 kJ mol$^{-1}$","464.5 kJ mol$^{-1}$","75 kJ mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $\\Delta_aH_1$ = 502 kJ mol$^{-1}$ and $\\Delta_aH_2$ = 427 kJ mol$^{-1}$.\nFormula: mean bond enthalpy = total dissociation enthalpy ÷ number of bonds broken.\nSubstituting: (502 + 427)/2 = 929/2.\nSo the mean O–H bond enthalpy = 464.5 kJ mol$^{-1}$.\nThe two values differ because the chemical environment changes after the first bond breaks.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-148","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":148,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the species: $B_2$, $C_2$, $N_2$, $O_2$, $O_2^+$, $O_2^-$, $O_2^{2-}$, $N_2^+$.\nHow many of these are paramagnetic and also have a bond order of 2 or more?","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Find the bond order (BO) and the number of unpaired electrons for each species:\n(i) $B_2$: BO 1, paramagnetic (fails the BO condition).\n(ii) $C_2$: BO 2, diamagnetic.\n(iii) $N_2$: BO 3, diamagnetic.\n(iv) $O_2$: BO 2, two unpaired $\\pi^*$ electrons, so it counts.\n(v) $O_2^+$: BO 2.5, one unpaired $\\pi^*$ electron, so it counts.\n(vi) $O_2^-$: BO 1.5, paramagnetic (BO too low).\n(vii) $O_2^{2-}$: BO 1, diamagnetic.\n(viii) $N_2^+$: 13 electrons, single $\\sigma2p_z$ electron, BO 2.5, paramagnetic, so it counts.\nThe species that qualify are $O_2$, $O_2^+$ and $N_2^+$, giving a total of 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-149","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":149,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"When one electron is removed from $N_2$ to form $N_2^+$, and from $O_2$ to form $O_2^+$, which statement correctly describes the change in bond strength? (Use the MO energy order given in NCERT for each molecule.)","image":"","options":["Both bonds become stronger because bond order increases in each case","The N–N bond becomes weaker while the O–O bond becomes stronger","The N–N bond becomes stronger while the O–O bond becomes weaker","Both bonds become weaker because an electron is lost in each case"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"For $N_2$, the order places $\\sigma 2p_z$ above the $\\pi 2p$ orbitals. The electron removed therefore comes from the bonding $\\sigma 2p_z$ orbital. Bond order falls from 3 to 2.5, so the bond becomes weaker and longer.\nFor $O_2$, the highest occupied orbitals are the antibonding $\\pi^*$ orbitals. Removing an electron from $\\pi^*$ raises the bond order from 2 to 2.5, so the bond becomes stronger and shorter.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-150","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":150,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"The water molecule has a net dipole moment of 1.85 D and an H–O–H bond angle of 104.5°. Treating the two O–H bond dipoles as equal vectors, what is the dipole moment of each O–H bond? (Take $\\cos 52.25^\\circ = 0.612$, $\\sin 52.25^\\circ = 0.791$.)","image":"","options":["1.51 D","0.93 D","1.17 D","3.02 D"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\mu_{net}$ = 1.85 D and bond angle 104.5°.\nFormula: $\\mu_{net} = 2\\mu_{OH}\\cos(\\theta/2)$.\nSubstituting: $\\mu_{OH} = \\frac{1.85}{2 \\times 0.612} = \\frac{1.85}{1.224}$.\nSo $\\mu_{OH} \\approx$ 1.51 D.\nOption 2 (0.93 D) simply halves 1.85 and ignores the vector angle.\nOption 3 (1.17 D) wrongly uses sin instead of cos.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-151","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":151,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the covalent character of ionic bonds (Fajans' rules) are correct?\n(i) The smaller the cation, the greater the covalent character of the ionic bond.\n(ii) The larger the anion, the greater the covalent character of the ionic bond.\n(iii) The greater the charge on the cation, the smaller the covalent character of the ionic bond.\n(iv) For cations of the same size and charge, one with an $(n-1)d^n ns^0$ configuration is more polarising than one with an $ns^2np^6$ configuration.\n(v) An anion of higher polarisability gives an ionic bond less covalent character.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: a small cation has high polarising power.\n(ii) Correct: a large anion is easily polarised.\n(iii) Incorrect: a higher cationic charge increases covalent character.\n(iv) Correct: cations typical of transition metals, with a $d^n$ configuration, are more polarising than those with a noble gas configuration.\n(v) Incorrect: a more polarisable anion is distorted more easily, which increases covalent character.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-152","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":152,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In the Lewis structure of $H_2SO_4$, the sulphur atom is surrounded by more than eight electrons.\nReason (R): The octet rule is based on the chemical inertness of noble gases.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true. S is a third-period element and can use d orbitals for bonding, so in $H_2SO_4$ it has an expanded octet (12 electrons).\nR is true. NCERT states that the octet rule is based on the inertness of noble gases.\nHowever, R only states what the octet rule is based on. It does not explain why S exceeds eight electrons; the availability of d orbitals does.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-153","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":153,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"The experimentally determined oxygen–oxygen bond length in the ozone ($O_3$) molecule is:","image":"","options":["121 pm","128 pm","148 pm","110 pm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT gives the normal O–O single bond as 148 pm and the O=O double bond as 121 pm.\nBoth O–O bonds in $O_3$ are found experimentally to be equal, at 128 pm.\nThis value lies between the single and double bond lengths, which is why ozone is described as a resonance hybrid.\n121 pm is tempting, but it is the length of a pure O=O bond.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-154","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":154,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about hybridisation is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The number of hybrid orbitals formed equals the number of atomic orbitals that hybridise.","Orbitals taking part in hybridisation should have almost equal energy.","Only half-filled orbitals of the valence shell can take part in hybridisation.","Promotion of an electron is not an essential condition before hybridisation."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: n atomic orbitals mix to give n hybrid orbitals.\n(2) Correct: the orbitals being mixed must be close in energy.\n(3) Incorrect: NCERT states that even filled valence-shell orbitals can take part. For example, the lone-pair orbitals of N in $NH_3$ are hybridised.\n(4) Correct: promotion of an electron is not a required step.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-155","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":155,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In the $PCl_5$ molecule, the two axial P–Cl bonds are slightly longer and weaker than the three equatorial P–Cl bonds.\nStatement II: Each axial bond pair experiences greater repulsion from the other bond pairs, because it lies at 90° to three equatorial bond pairs.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. In trigonal bipyramidal $PCl_5$ (sp$^3$d hybridisation), the axial bonds are found to be slightly longer than the equatorial bonds. This makes $PCl_5$ fairly reactive.\nStatement II is correct. An axial bond pair has three neighbouring bond pairs at 90°. An equatorial bond pair has only two neighbours at 90° (the axial ones) and the others at 120°. The greater repulsion on the axial bonds lengthens them.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-156","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":156,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about resonance are correct?\n(i) The resonance hybrid has lower energy than any single canonical structure.\n(ii) Canonical forms have real existence and can be isolated under suitable conditions.\n(iii) Resonance averages the bond characteristics of the molecule as a whole.\n(iv) The actual structure of a molecule showing resonance can be depicted exactly by one of its Lewis structures.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct. Resonance stabilises a molecule because the hybrid is lower in energy than every canonical form.\n(ii) Incorrect. Canonical forms have no real existence.\n(iii) Correct. Bond lengths and other bond characteristics become averaged, as in $O_3$ and $CO_3^{2-}$.\n(iv) Incorrect. The hybrid cannot be shown by any single Lewis structure.\nSo 2 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-157","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":157,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the species $O_2^+$, $O_2$, $O_2^-$ and $O_2^{2-}$. Which option correctly gives the order of O–O bond length and identifies the diamagnetic species?","image":"","options":["$O_2^{2-} > O_2^- > O_2 > O_2^+$; only $O_2^{2-}$ is diamagnetic","$O_2^+ > O_2 > O_2^- > O_2^{2-}$; only $O_2^{2-}$ is diamagnetic","$O_2^{2-} > O_2^- > O_2 > O_2^+$; both $O_2^-$ and $O_2^{2-}$ are diamagnetic","$O_2 > O_2^- > O_2^+ > O_2^{2-}$; only $O_2$ is diamagnetic"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Bond order = ($N_b - N_a$)/2. With $N_b$ = 10, the antibonding electron counts are 5, 6, 7 and 8.\nThis gives bond orders: $O_2^+$ = 2.5, $O_2$ = 2, $O_2^-$ = 1.5, $O_2^{2-}$ = 1.\nBond length increases as bond order decreases, so $O_2^{2-} > O_2^- > O_2 > O_2^+$.\nUnpaired $\\pi^*$ electrons: $O_2^+$ has 1, $O_2$ has 2, $O_2^-$ has 1 and $O_2^{2-}$ has 0. So only $O_2^{2-}$ is diamagnetic.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-158","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":158,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following molecules contains two double bonds?","image":"","options":["$C_2H_4$","$CO_2$","$C_2H_2$","$N_2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In $CO_2$ (O=C=O), carbon shares two electron pairs with each oxygen. This gives two double bonds and completes the octet on all three atoms.\n$C_2H_4$ has only one C=C double bond.\n$C_2H_2$ and $N_2$ each have a triple bond, with three shared pairs.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-159","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":159,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following molecules has polar bonds but a net dipole moment of zero?","image":"","options":["$NH_3$","$H_2O$","$BF_3$","$NF_3$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Net dipole moment is the vector sum of the bond dipoles, so it depends on molecular shape.\n$BF_3$ is trigonal planar with B–F bonds at $120^\\circ$. The resultant of any two bond moments is equal and opposite to the third, so $\\mu = 0$.\n$NH_3$ and $NF_3$ are pyramidal, so their bond moments do not cancel ($\\mu \\neq 0$).\n$H_2O$ is bent ($\\mu$ = 1.85 D).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-160","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":160,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following species are exceptions to the octet rule (central atom with an incomplete octet, an odd number of electrons, or an expanded octet)?\n$BeH_2$, $BCl_3$, $NO_2$, $SF_6$, $H_2SO_4$, $CO_2$, $SCl_2$, $CH_4$","image":"","options":["4","5","6","7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Incomplete octet: $BeH_2$ (Be has 4 electrons) and $BCl_3$ (B has 6 electrons).\nOdd-electron molecule: $NO_2$.\nExpanded octet: $SF_6$ (12 electrons on S) and $H_2SO_4$ (S beyond an octet, as per NCERT).\nOctet obeyed: $CO_2$, $SCl_2$ (S has an octet) and $CH_4$.\nSo there are 5 exceptions.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-161","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":161,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"During the formation of $H_2$ from atoms A and B, consider the following interactions:\n$N_A - e_A$, $N_B - e_B$, $N_A - e_B$, $N_B - e_A$, $e_A - e_B$, $N_A - N_B$\nHow many of these are attractive interactions?","image":"","options":["2","3","4","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Each nucleus attracts its own electron: $N_A-e_A$ and $N_B-e_B$ (2 interactions).\nThe new attractions are between each nucleus and the other atom's electron: $N_A-e_B$ and $N_B-e_A$ (2 interactions).\nElectron–electron ($e_A-e_B$) and nucleus–nucleus ($N_A-N_B$) interactions are repulsive.\nTotal attractive interactions = 4.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-162","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":162,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about the ethene molecule, $C_2H_4$:\n(i) Both carbon atoms are $sp^2$ hybridised.\n(ii) The electron clouds of the $\\pi$ bond lie above and below the plane containing the carbon and hydrogen atoms.\n(iii) The H–C–H bond angle is exactly $120^\\circ$.\n(iv) Each C–H bond is an $sp^2$–s sigma bond.\nHow many of these statements are correct?","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: each carbon uses three $sp^2$ hybrids.\n(ii) Correct: the $\\pi$ bond forms from sideways overlap of unhybridised 2p orbitals that are perpendicular to the molecular plane. Its electron clouds therefore lie above and below that plane.\n(iii) Incorrect: NCERT gives the H–C–H angle as $117.6^\\circ$ and the H–C–C angle as $121^\\circ$, not an ideal $120^\\circ$.\n(iv) Correct: each C–H bond forms from an $sp^2$ hybrid overlapping with H 1s.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-163","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":163,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Liquid hydrogen fluoride\n(B) Ethanol dissolved in water\n(C) o-Nitrophenol\n(D) Solid line in H–F····H–F\nList-II\n(i) Intramolecular hydrogen bond\n(ii) Intermolecular hydrogen bond between molecules of the same compound\n(iii) Intermolecular hydrogen bond between molecules of different compounds\n(iv) Covalent bond","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) HF–HF: hydrogen bonds form between molecules of the same compound, so (ii).\n(B) Ethanol–water: hydrogen bonds form between molecules of two different compounds, so (iii).\n(C) o-Nitrophenol: the OH hydrogen bonds to the nitro oxygen within the same molecule, so (i).\n(D) A solid line represents a covalent bond, so (iv).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-164","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":164,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"The arrangement of $Na^+$ and $Cl^-$ ions in crystalline sodium chloride is commonly known as the:","image":"","options":["Diamond structure","Rock salt structure","Zinc blende structure","Cubical octet structure"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT shows the crystal structure of sodium chloride and calls it the rock salt structure.\nIn this structure, $Na^+$ and $Cl^-$ ions are arranged in an orderly three-dimensional pattern held together by coulombic attraction.\nThe 'cubical octet' is Lewis's model of eight electrons at the corners of a cube around an atom. It describes electrons in an atom, not a crystal lattice.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-165","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":165,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about resonance is correct?","image":"","options":["The resonance hybrid has lower energy than any single canonical structure","Each canonical form has real existence for a short fraction of time","The canonical forms are in dynamic equilibrium like keto and enol tautomers","The molecule keeps switching between its canonical forms"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Resonance stabilises a molecule because the hybrid is lower in energy than any single canonical structure. So option (1) is correct.\nThe canonical forms have no real existence, which rules out option (2).\nThere is no equilibrium between canonical forms as there is between tautomers, which rules out option (3).\nThe molecule does not oscillate between forms; it has a single hybrid structure, which rules out option (4).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-166","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":166,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"For the Lewis structure of the nitrite ion, $NO_2^-$, with one N=O bond, one N–O bond, a lone pair on N and octets on all atoms, which statement is correct?","image":"","options":["The nitrogen atom carries a formal charge of −1","The doubly bonded oxygen carries a formal charge of −1","Only the singly bonded oxygen carries a non-zero formal charge, equal to −1","The structure uses 17 valence electrons in total"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Total valence electrons = 5 + 12 + 1 = 18, so (4) is wrong.\nN: 5 − 2 − ½(6) = 0, so (1) is wrong.\nDoubly bonded O: 6 − 4 − ½(4) = 0, so (2) is wrong.\nSingly bonded O: 6 − 6 − ½(2) = −1.\nThe sum of formal charges is −1, equal to the ionic charge, so (3) is correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-167","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":167,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"The ionization enthalpy for $Na(g) \\rightarrow Na^+(g) + e^-$ is 495.8 kJ mol$^{-1}$. The electron gain enthalpy for $Cl(g) + e^- \\rightarrow Cl^-(g)$ is $-348.7$ kJ mol$^{-1}$. What is the net enthalpy change for $Na(g) + Cl(g) \\rightarrow Na^+(g) + Cl^-(g)$?","image":"","options":["+147.1 kJ mol$^{-1}$","+844.5 kJ mol$^{-1}$","$-147.1$ kJ mol$^{-1}$","$-640.9$ kJ mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: ionization enthalpy = +495.8 kJ mol$^{-1}$ and electron gain enthalpy = $-348.7$ kJ mol$^{-1}$.\nFormula: $\\Delta H = \\Delta_i H + \\Delta_{eg} H$\nSubstituting: $\\Delta H = 495.8 + (-348.7)$\nSo $\\Delta H$ = +147.1 kJ mol$^{-1}$. Forming the gaseous ions absorbs energy.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-168","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":168,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The double bond in the $C_2$ molecule consists of two $\\pi$ bonds and no $\\sigma$ bond.\nReason (R): In $C_2$, the $\\pi2p_x$ and $\\pi2p_y$ molecular orbitals are lower in energy than $\\sigma2p_z$. The four electrons left after filling $\\sigma1s$, $\\sigma^*1s$, $\\sigma2s$ and $\\sigma^*2s$ therefore all go into the two $\\pi$ bonding orbitals.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"$C_2$ has 12 electrons. For $C_2$, the $\\sigma2p_z$ orbital lies above the $\\pi2p$ orbitals.\nConfiguration: $KK\\,\\sigma2s^2\\,\\sigma^*2s^2\\,\\pi2p_x^2\\,\\pi2p_y^2$.\nBond order $= \\frac{1}{2}(8-4) = 2$, and both bonding pairs beyond $\\sigma2s$ sit in $\\pi$ MOs.\nA is true: NCERT states that the double bond in $C_2$ is made up of two pi bonds.\nR is true, and it is exactly why no $\\sigma2p_z$ electron is present. R therefore correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-169","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":169,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Which hybridisation and shape does NCERT assign to the complex ion $[Ni(CN)_4]^{2-}$?","image":"","options":["$sp^3$, tetrahedral","$sp^3d$, trigonal bipyramidal","$dsp^2$, square planar","$d^2sp^3$, octahedral"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT lists $[Ni(CN)_4]^{2-}$ and $[PtCl_4]^{2-}$ as examples of $dsp^2$ hybridisation.\nThis scheme uses one d, one s and two p orbitals, giving four hybrids that point to the corners of a square.\nThe tempting option, tetrahedral $sp^3$, is wrong because one d orbital is involved here.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-170","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":170,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Using VSEPR theory and the bond angles given in NCERT, arrange the following in decreasing order of bond angle at the central atom:\n$CH_4$, $NH_3$, $H_2O$, $BF_3$, $SO_2$","image":"","options":["$SO_2 > BF_3 > CH_4 > NH_3 > H_2O$","$BF_3 > CH_4 > SO_2 > NH_3 > H_2O$","$BF_3 > SO_2 > CH_4 > NH_3 > H_2O$","$CH_4 > BF_3 > SO_2 > NH_3 > H_2O$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"$BF_3$: trigonal planar with no lone pair, so the angle is 120°.\n$SO_2$: trigonal planar electron arrangement with one lone pair, so lp–bp repulsion reduces the angle to 119.5°.\n$CH_4$: regular tetrahedron with an angle of 109.5°.\n$NH_3$: one lone pair reduces the angle to 107°.\n$H_2O$: two lone pairs reduce the angle further to 104.5°.\nThe decreasing order is therefore $BF_3 > SO_2 > CH_4 > NH_3 > H_2O$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-171","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":171,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following molecules have at least one lone pair of electrons on the central atom?\n$SO_2$, $NH_3$, $BF_3$, $CH_4$, $SF_4$, $XeF_4$, $PCl_5$, $BrF_5$, $H_2O$, $BeCl_2$","image":"","options":["4","5","6","7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Molecules with a lone pair on the central atom:\n$SO_2$ (1 lp), $NH_3$ (1 lp), $SF_4$ (1 lp), $XeF_4$ (2 lp), $BrF_5$ (1 lp) and $H_2O$ (2 lp). That makes 6.\nMolecules with no lone pair on the central atom: $BF_3$, $CH_4$, $PCl_5$ and $BeCl_2$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-172","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":172,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (species) with List-II (bond order and magnetic behaviour) according to MO theory:\nList-I: (a) $O_2^+$ (b) $O_2^-$ (c) $O_2^{2-}$ (d) $N_2$\nList-II: (i) bond order 1, diamagnetic (ii) bond order 2.5, paramagnetic (iii) bond order 3, diamagnetic (iv) bond order 1.5, paramagnetic","image":"","options":["a-iv, b-ii, c-i, d-iii","a-ii, b-i, c-iv, d-iii","a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii","a-ii, b-iv, c-i, d-iii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Neutral $O_2$ has 10 bonding and 6 antibonding electrons (bond order 2), with two unpaired electrons in the $\\pi^*$ orbitals.\n(a) $O_2^+$: one $\\pi^*$ electron is removed, giving $\\frac{10-5}{2}=2.5$. One unpaired electron remains, so it is paramagnetic → (ii).\n(b) $O_2^-$: one $\\pi^*$ electron is added, giving $\\frac{10-7}{2}=1.5$. One unpaired electron, so paramagnetic → (iv).\n(c) $O_2^{2-}$: $\\frac{10-8}{2}=1$. All electrons are paired, so diamagnetic → (i).\n(d) $N_2$: $\\frac{10-4}{2}=3$. All electrons are paired, so diamagnetic → (iii).\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-173","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":173,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"A hydrogen atom can take part in hydrogen bonding when it is covalently bonded to a highly electronegative atom. Which set contains only such atoms?","image":"","options":["F, O and N","F, Cl and Br","C, N and O","S, O and Cl"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT names nitrogen, oxygen and fluorine as the highly electronegative elements that give hydrogen bonds when attached to H.\nIn N–H, O–H and F–H bonds the shared pair shifts strongly towards N, O or F, leaving H with a partial positive charge.\nOption (2) includes Cl and Br, and options (3) and (4) include C, S and Cl. These are not the atoms NCERT lists for hydrogen bonding.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-174","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":174,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: $N_2$, CO and $NO^+$ are isoelectronic and all have a bond order of 3.\nStatement II: As the bond order increases, the bond length increases and the bond enthalpy decreases.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: isoelectronic species have identical bond orders, and $N_2$, CO and $NO^+$ each have a triple bond (bond order 3).\nStatement II is incorrect: the general correlation is the reverse. Higher bond order gives a larger bond enthalpy and a shorter bond.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-175","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":175,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"In each canonical form of ozone, the central O carries a formal charge of +1, one terminal O carries −1 and the other terminal O carries 0. In the resonance hybrid, what is the effective (averaged) formal charge on each terminal oxygen atom?","image":"","options":["0","$-\\frac{1}{2}$","$-1$","$+\\frac{1}{2}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Formal charge of O = 6 − (lone-pair electrons) − ½(bonding electrons).\nThe two canonical forms differ only in which terminal O carries −1. That O has 3 lone pairs and one single bond.\nThe hybrid averages the two forms, so each terminal O gets (−1 + 0)/2 = −½.\nThe central O stays at +1, and the total charge is +1 − ½ − ½ = 0.\nThis averaging matches the two equal O–O bonds found by experiment.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-176","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":176,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following species have fewer than eight electrons around the central atom (or the metal atom) in their Lewis structures?\nLiCl, BeH$_2$, BCl$_3$, CO$_2$, PF$_5$, H$_2$SO$_4$, SCl$_2$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"LiCl: Li has only 2 electrons in its bond → incomplete octet.\nBeH$_2$: Be has 4 electrons around it → incomplete octet.\nBCl$_3$: B has 6 electrons around it → incomplete octet.\nCO$_2$ and SCl$_2$: the octet is obeyed.\nPF$_5$ and H$_2$SO$_4$: the central atom has more than 8 electrons (expanded octet).\nThat makes 3 species.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-177","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":177,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"How many sigma ($\\sigma$) and pi ($\\pi$) bonds are present in one molecule of ethyne, $C_2H_2$?","image":"","options":["2 $\\sigma$ and 3 $\\pi$","3 $\\sigma$ and 2 $\\pi$","4 $\\sigma$ and 1 $\\pi$","5 $\\sigma$ and 0 $\\pi$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Each carbon in ethyne is sp hybridised.\nAxial overlap of sp orbitals gives one C–C $\\sigma$ bond.\nAxial overlap of sp orbitals with H 1s orbitals gives two C–H $\\sigma$ bonds.\nSideways overlap of the unhybridised $2p_y$ and $2p_z$ orbitals gives two $\\pi$ bonds.\nTotal: 3 $\\sigma$ and 2 $\\pi$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-BONDING-178","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","qno":178,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (species) with List-II (shape) and choose the correct option.\nList-I:\n(A) $[Ni(CN)_4]^{2-}$\n(B) $PCl_5$\n(C) $BrF_5$\n(D) $SF_6$\nList-II:\n(i) Octahedral\n(ii) Square pyramidal\n(iii) Square planar\n(iv) Trigonal bipyramidal","image":"","options":["A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(A) $[Ni(CN)_4]^{2-}$ is $dsp^2$ hybridised, so it is square planar → (iii).\n(B) $PCl_5$ is $sp^3d$ hybridised, so it is trigonal bipyramidal → (iv).\n(C) $BrF_5$ is $sp^3d^2$ with one lone pair, so it is square pyramidal → (ii).\n(D) $SF_6$ is $sp^3d^2$ with no lone pair, so it is octahedral → (i).\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 4 · Chemical Bonding and Molecular Structure","difficulty":"easy"}]});
